(Chile 2001)_01,C_EXPRESS_ORDER,False,,True,1.0,"Article 8.- A media outlet that disseminates informative material identifying it as authored by a specific journalist or person, with their name, face or voice, shall not introduce substantial alterations to it without the consent of that person; the media outlet shall be responsible for such alterations and, at the request of the affected person, shall make the corresponding clarification. This right of the affected person shall lapse if it is not exercised within the six days following.

Article 8.- A violation of the provisions of the preceding paragraphs, when the affected person is a journalist employed or contracted to perform journalistic functions by the respective media outlet, shall constitute a serious breach by the employer of the obligations imposed by the employment contract.

Article 10.- To hold the positions of head of press or journalist, when so required by the respective establishment, in any body of the centralized or decentralized administration of the State, or in any of its companies, it shall be required to be in possession of the journalist degree, in accordance with the provisions of the preceding paragraph.

Article 12.- On the first page or on the editorial page or on the last page, and always in a prominent place of every daily, magazine or periodic writing, and at the beginning and end of the daily transmissions of every free-to-air sound broadcasting or television service or limited television services, the name and domicile of the owner or concessionaire, as the case may be, or of the legal representative, if it is a legal person, shall be indicated. The same mentions shall be made regarding the responsible director.

Article 13.- Every printed matter, sound recording or audiovisual or electronic production made in the country and intended for commercialization shall include the name of the person responsible or establishment that carried out the printing or production, as well as the corresponding place and date, without prejudice to complying, as appropriate, with the other requirements established by law. In the case of books, the number of copies shall be placed in a visible place.

Article 14.- The obligation established in this article shall be fulfilled within a maximum period of ninety days. In case of non-compliance with the provisions of the first and third paragraphs, together with the corresponding complaint, the delivery of the copies may be demanded from the natural or legal person responsible for their editorial production, within sixty days counted from the expiration of the previous period.

Article 15.- Free-to-air sound broadcasting or television services and limited television services, regarding their programs of national origin, shall be obliged to leave a copy or magnetic tape and to keep it for twenty days of every news, interview, talk, commentary, conference, speech, editorial, discourse or debate that they have broadcast.

Article 17.- A person offended or unjustly alluded to by a free-to-air sound broadcasting or television service or a limited television service shall have the right, paying only the cost of the material used in reproduction or providing that which will be used for it, to directly request the delivery of a faithful copy of the transmission referred to in Article 15, which shall be made available to them within the fifth day.

Article 20.- The right referred to in this Title shall prescribe within twenty days, counted from the date of the issuance. It may only be exercised by the person offended or unjustly alluded to, or by their agent or attorney, or, in the event of death or absence of the latter, by their spouse or by their relatives by consanguinity or affinity up to the second degree inclusive.

Article 30.- Evidence of truth regarding their expressions shall not be admitted to the accused of having caused injury through a media outlet, except when they have imputed specific facts and at least one of the following circumstances concurred:

Article 30 a) Evidence of truth regarding their expressions shall not be admitted to the accused of having caused injury through a media outlet, except when they have imputed specific facts and at least one of the following circumstances concurred: a) That the imputation was made for the purpose of defending a real public interest;

Article 30 b) Evidence of truth regarding their expressions shall not be admitted to the accused of having caused injury through a media outlet, except when they have imputed specific facts and at least one of the following circumstances concurred: b) That the affected person exercised public functions and the imputation refers to facts inherent to such exercise.

Article 30.- In these cases, if the truth of the imputation is proved, the judge shall proceed to definitively dismiss or acquit the accused, as appropriate.

Article 30.- For the purposes of this Article, the following shall be considered facts of public interest of a person: a) Those relating to the performance of public functions;

Article 30 b) For the purposes of this Article, the following shall be considered facts of public interest of a person: b) Those carried out in the exercise of a profession or trade and whose knowledge has a real public interest;

Article 30 c) For the purposes of this Article, the following shall be considered facts of public interest of a person: c) Those that consist of activities to which the public has had free access, for free or for a fee;

Article 30 d) For the purposes of this Article, the following shall be considered facts of public interest of a person: d) Acts that, with the consent of the interested party, have been captured or disseminated by any media outlet;

Article 30 e) For the purposes of this Article, the following shall be considered facts of public interest of a person: e) Events or statements of which the interested party has left a record in public registries or archives;

Article 33.- The disclosure, by any media outlet, of the identity of minors who are perpetrators, accomplices, coverers or witnesses of crimes, or any other antecedent that leads to it, is prohibited.

Article 33.- This prohibition shall also apply to the victims of the crimes contemplated in Articles 141, final paragraph; 142, final paragraph; 150 A, 150 D, of some of the crimes contemplated in Title Seven of Book Two, ""Crimes and offenses against family order, public morality and sexual integrity"" and of the crimes contemplated in Articles 411 quater, when committed for purposes of sexual exploitation, and 433, number 1, in relation to rape, all of the Penal Code, unless they expressly consent to the disclosure.

Article 42.- Whenever one of the offended parties so demands, the trial court shall order the dissemination, in extract drafted by the court clerk, of the conviction rendered in a process for any of the crimes referred to in paragraph 3 of Title IV of this law, in the media outlet in which the infraction occurred, at the offender's expense.

Article 46 a) In Law No. 12,927 on State Security, letter b) of Article 6 is replaced by the following: ""b) Those who publicly affront the flag, the coat of arms, the name of the homeland or the national anthem;""."
