(Chile 2001)_01,GOV_REGULATOR,False,,True,1.0,"Article 6.- While they carry out the professional internships required by their institutions, students of journalism schools, and graduates of the same, until twenty-four months after their graduation date, shall have the rights and shall be subject to the responsibilities that this law contemplates for journalists.

Article 7.- Directors, editors of media outlets, the persons referred to in Articles 5 and 6 and foreign correspondents who carry out their activity in the country shall have the right to maintain confidentiality regarding their information source, which shall extend to the elements in their possession that allow its identification, and they shall not be compelled to reveal it, not even judicially.

Article 7.- The provision in the preceding paragraph shall also apply to persons who, by virtue of their office or informative activity, necessarily had to be present at the moment the information was received.

Article 9.- Concessions for free-to-air sound broadcasting requested by legal persons with foreign capital participation exceeding ten percent may only be granted if it is previously proven that in their country of origin rights and obligations similar to the conditions that these applicants will enjoy in Chile are granted to Chileans. The same requirement shall be fulfilled to acquire an existing concession. Failure to comply with this condition shall result in the automatic termination of the concession.

Article 10.- Media outlets shall have a responsible director and at least one person to replace them.

Article 10.- The director and those who replace them shall be Chilean, have domicile and residence in the country, shall not have immunity, shall be in full enjoyment of their civil and political rights, shall not have been convicted of a crime that merits an afflictive penalty and, in the last two years, shall not have been convicted as authors of repeated crimes or as recidivists in crimes penalized by this law. A conviction imposing an afflictive penalty shall immediately terminate the affected person in all functions or activities related to the administration of the media outlet.

Article 11.- The commencement of activities of written media outlets shall be reported to the Provincial Governor's Office or Regional Intendancy corresponding to the domicile of the medium by means of a declaration, of which that Governor's Office or Intendancy shall send a copy to the Director of the National Library. The declaration shall contain the following statements:

Article 11 a) The commencement of activities of written media outlets shall be reported to the Provincial Governor's Office or Regional Intendancy corresponding to the domicile of the medium by means of a declaration, of which that Governor's Office or Intendancy shall send a copy to the Director of the National Library. The declaration shall contain the following statements: a) The name of the daily, magazine or periodical, indicating the periods that will elapse between one issue and another;

Article 11 c) The commencement of activities of written media outlets shall be reported to the Provincial Governor's Office or Regional Intendancy corresponding to the domicile of the medium by means of a declaration, of which that Governor's Office or Intendancy shall send a copy to the Director of the National Library. The declaration shall contain the following statements: c) The full name, domicile and the documents proving the identity of the director and of the person or persons who must replace them, indicating the order of precedence in which they must assume replacement;

Article 11 d) The commencement of activities of written media outlets shall be reported to the Provincial Governor's Office or Regional Intendancy corresponding to the domicile of the medium by means of a declaration, of which that Governor's Office or Intendancy shall send a copy to the Director of the National Library. The declaration shall contain the following statements: d) The location of its main offices;

Article 14.- In the case of periodical publications, the Director of the National Library shall be empowered to enter into agreements with the persons responsible for such media to establish modalities of mixed legal deposit, reducing the number of paper copies, substituting the remainder with reproductions of them on microfilms and/or electronic media.

Article 14.- In the case of printed publications in regions, of the five copies, two of them shall be deposited in the public library of the region designated by the Director of the National Library.

Article 14.- The National Library may reject and require a new copy if any of the deposited copies, in any medium, exhibit deficiencies or some deterioration that prevents their consultation or preservation.

Article 14.- In the case of sound recordings or audiovisual or electronic productions intended for commercialization, such persons or establishments shall deposit two copies of each.

Article 14.- The obligation established in this article shall be fulfilled within a maximum period of thirty days.

Article 14.- In the case of cinematographic creations, the obligation shall be understood to be fulfilled by depositing one copy in original format and one in digital format in the National Film Archive and another, also in digital format, in the National Library.

Article 14.- State organisms shall not be able to acquire printed works, sound recordings or electronic or audiovisual, film or cinematographic productions from editors, producers or creators who do not comply with the obligation established in this article, nor grant financing to them through funds or subsidies. For these purposes, the National Library shall certify that it has not made complaints relating to this infringement.

Article 17.- In the event that the respective service does not deliver the copy within the time limit or unjustifiably refuses to do so, and a criminal judge deems it pertinent to establish a possible criminal fact, at the request of the interested party and at their expense, they may request the sending of the copy to make it available to them. The responsible director or the person who replaces them shall deliver the faithful copy of the transmission to the court within the third day counted from the notification of the resolution ordering its sending.

Article 18.- This request shall be addressed to its director, or to the person who must replace them, within the period of twenty days, counted from the date of the edition or broadcast that motivates it.

Article 18.- Notaries and court receivers shall be obliged to notify the request at the simple request of the interested party. The notification shall be made by means of a notice that shall contain the full text of the clarification or rectification, which shall be delivered to the director or to the person who legally replaces them, at the legally constituted domicile.

Article 19.- The broadcast intended to rectify or clarify shall be made, at the latest, in the first edition or transmission that meets the indicated characteristics and that takes place after the twenty-four hours following the delivery of the originals that contain it. If it is a publication that does not appear every day, the clarification or rectification shall be delivered with at least seventy-two hours' notice.

Article 19.- If the medium makes new comments on the clarification or rectification, the affected person shall have the right to reply according to the foregoing rules. In any case, the comments shall be made in such a way that they are clearly distinguished from the clarification or rectification.

Article 22.- Infractions to Title III shall be sanctioned with a fine of two to thirty monthly tax units. In addition, in its judgment, the court shall set a period for the defendant to fully comply with the rule infringed, if appropriate.

Article 23.- The knowledge and resolution of complaints for these infractions shall correspond to the civil court of first instance of the domicile of the media outlet.

Article 24.- The procedure shall be subject to the following rules:

Article 24 a) The procedure shall be subject to the following rules: a) The complaint shall clearly indicate the infraction committed, the facts that configure it and attach the means of proof that substantiate them, if applicable.

Article 24 b) The procedure shall be subject to the following rules: b) The court shall order that it be notified in accordance with the provisions of the final paragraph of Article 18. In the same manner the judgment that is rendered shall be notified.

Article 24 c) The procedure shall be subject to the following rules: c) The defendant shall submit their pleadings within the fifth working day and attach the means of proof that substantiate the facts on which they are based. If they do not have them, they shall state this circumstance and the court shall set a hearing, within the fifth working day, in order to receive the evidence offered and not attached.

Article 24 e) The procedure shall be subject to the following rules: e) The resolutions shall be issued in a single instance and shall be notified by the daily bulletin.

Article 24 f) The procedure shall be subject to the following rules: f) The final judgment shall be appealable in both effects. The appeal must be filed within the fatal term of five days, counted from the notification of the party that files it, must contain the grounds of fact and law on which it is based and the specific petitions made. Once the appeal is filed, the court shall immediately forward the records to the Court of Appeals. It shall decide in chambers, without awaiting the appearance of any of the parties, within six working days following the date of entry of the file to the court registry.

Article 26.- The knowledge and resolution of complaints or criminal charges for infractions to Title IV shall correspond to the criminal court with jurisdiction over the domicile of the media outlet.

Article 27.- The procedure shall be subject to the rules established in Article 24, with the following modifications: a) The period to submit pleadings shall be three working days, and b) There shall be no special term for evidence.

Article 28.- When, by application of the provisions of this article, a media outlet is temporarily suspended, its staff shall receive, during the period of suspension, all the remunerations to which they are legally or contractually entitled, under the same conditions as if they were in function.

Article 32.- Exempted from the preceding paragraph are specialized legal publications, which shall not give rise to civil or criminal liability for the dissemination of news or information on proceedings or judicial actions that are related or, if they are pending, provided that the interested parties are not identified.

Article 33.- To report the investigation or trial, reference shall be made to the victim either by their initials, a number or any other manner that does not enable their identification. In addition, the use of any editorial resource that accounts for stereotypes or prejudices regarding their condition as a victim, possible responsibility in the facts, previous or subsequent conduct to the crime or any other element that normalizes, justifies or relativizes the violence suffered shall be avoided.

Article 38.- Any fact or act relevant relating to the modification or change in the ownership of a media outlet shall be reported to the National Economic Prosecutor's Office within thirty days of being carried out.

Article 38.- However, in the case of media outlets subject to the concession system granted by the State, the relevant fact or act must have, prior to its perfection, a report from the National Economic Prosecutor's Office regarding its effect on competition, which must be issued within thirty days following receipt of the background. If the report is unfavorable, the National Economic Prosecutor must communicate it to the Court for the purposes of the provisions of Article 31 of Decree with Force of Law No. 1, of 2005, of the Ministry of Economy, Development and Reconstruction. If the report is not issued within said period, it shall be understood that it does not warrant any objection by the Prosecutor's Office.

Article 39.- Criminal and civil liability for the crimes and abuses committed in the exercise of the freedoms enshrined in the first paragraph of number 12 of Article 19 of the Political Constitution of the Republic shall be determined by the provisions of this law and those of the respective Codes.

Article 42.- If the publication is not made within the period indicated by the court, the provisions of the second paragraph of Article 28 shall apply."
