(Venezuela 2000)_02,GOV_OVERSIGHT,False,,True,1.0,"ARTICLE 11.- Before producing or modifying the normative acts that it may issue in accordance with this Law, the National Telecommunications Commission shall carry out prior public consultations with the interested sectors. For such purposes it shall establish by resolution the mechanisms that allow ensuring timely information to the interested parties and the possibility that they provide suggestions or recommendations, under the terms and conditions that are determined, for which it shall seek the establishment of open, electronic or audiovisual mechanisms. Natural or legal persons may propose to the National Telecommunications Commission the regulation of new telecommunications services.

ARTICLE 132.- Upon receipt of the request by the operator to whom interconnection was requested, the parties shall, by mutual agreement, determine the negotiation mechanisms they consider appropriate and the period in which they propose to reach an agreement, which may not exceed sixty continuous days counted from the date on which the request was received.

ARTICLE 132.- In the interconnection agreement the parties shall provide with full precision the term and the modalities under which the agreement will be executed.

ARTICLE 133.- When the parties do not reach an agreement within the period provided for, the National Telecommunications Commission shall have a period not greater than thirty continuous days, extendable for an equal period, to order that the requested interconnection be made effective and to establish the technical and economic conditions of the same. The action of said Commission, in this case, shall be strictly necessary to protect the interests of users and shall be carried out ex officio, or at the instance of both interested parties or of one of them, and its decision shall be issued after hearing the parties concerned.

ARTICLE 135.- Any controversy arising in relation to an interconnection contract shall be resolved between the parties, in accordance with the terms established in the corresponding contract. In case the parties do not reach the agreement that ends the controversy, it shall be submitted by one or both parties, through a motivated communication, to the National Telecommunications Commission, which must decide in a reasoned manner within a period of thirty continuous days counted from its presentation, once the arguments and evidence of the parties have been presented. In said term the National Telecommunications Commission may carry out inspections or audits as well as request any other complementary information that is pertinent for the resolution of the matter in dispute."
