(Venezuela 2000)_02,GOV_REGULATOR,True,1.0,True,1.0,"ARTICLE 15.- Telecommunications service operators, duly accredited, have the duty to: 1. Respect the rights of users established in the Constitution and in the law, to adequate and non-misleading information about the content and characteristics of the products and services they consume, to freedom of choice and to equitable and dignified treatment.

ARTICLE 15.- Telecommunications service operators, duly accredited, have the duty to: 2. Respect the minimum quality conditions established by the National Telecommunications Commission, in the provision of their services, in accordance with the regulations of this Law;

ARTICLE 15.- Telecommunications service operators, duly accredited, have the duty to: 5. Publish the maximum prices of the services they provide to users, with at least fifteen continuous days' notice prior to their entry into force, in newspapers with the greatest circulation in the geographic area in which they operate or, failing that, in newspapers of national circulation, and notify the National Telecommunications Commission, within that same period, of the maximum prices of the services referred to.

ARTICLE 15.- Telecommunications service operators, duly accredited, have the duty to: 6. Comply with the decisions that, in accordance with this Law and its regulations, are issued by the National Telecommunications Commission;

ARTICLE 15.- Telecommunications service operators, duly accredited, have the duty to: 10. Comply with the obligations of assistance, provision of services, supply and provision of goods and resources, and with all those obligations that are established in the applicable regulations to telecommunications services in states of exception, and in the plans for states of exception that are formulated for that purpose.

ARTICLE 23.- Administrative authorization shall not be required for the installation or operation of telecommunications equipment or networks, in the following cases: 2. When they are equipment that, despite using portions of the radioelectric spectrum, have been classified as free use by the National Telecommunications Commission;

ARTICLE 23.- Administrative authorization shall not be required for the installation or operation of telecommunications equipment or networks, in the following cases: 3. When they are telecommunications equipment or networks of the organs of the Republic, of the states or of the municipalities, when such activities are carried out to satisfy their communicational needs, without third-party economic consideration nor use of the public radioelectric domain.

ARTICLE 23.- Administrative authorization shall not be required for the installation or operation of telecommunications equipment or networks, in the following cases: 4. When they are services that use as support networks, links or telecommunications systems, in order to offer additional facilities to those defined as attributes of the administrative authorizations, applying to these facilities processes that make possible the availability of information, action upon them or interaction with the system. Internet service providers are excepted.

ARTICLE 23.- Administrative authorization shall not be required for the installation or operation of telecommunications equipment or networks, in the following cases: Sole Paragraph: By regulation it may be established the cases and modalities in which the assumptions provided for in this article will require notification to the National Telecommunications Commission or prior registration of the respective project.

ARTICLE 49.- The State guarantees the provision of the Universal Telecommunications Service. The Universal Telecommunications Service is the defined set of telecommunications services that operators are obliged to provide to users in order to offer them minimum standards of penetration, access, quality and economic affordability regardless of geographic location.

ARTICLE 50.- The National Telecommunications Commission, in coordination with the Ministry of Infrastructure, shall establish as a priority for the purpose of progressively achieving the obligations of the Universal Service the following provisions: 1. That all persons may receive connection to the public fixed telephone network and access the provision of the public fixed telephone service available to the public. The connection must offer the user the possibility to make and receive national and international calls and allow the transmission of voice, text and data.

ARTICLE 50.- The National Telecommunications Commission, in coordination with the Ministry of Infrastructure, shall establish as a priority for the purpose of progressively achieving the obligations of the Universal Service the following provisions: 2. That subscribers to the telephone service have, free of charge, an updated, printed and unified telephone directory for each territorial scope. All subscribers shall have the right to be listed in such directories and to a national information service about their content, without prejudice, in any case, to the respect for the rules that regulate the protection of personal data and the right to privacy.

ARTICLE 50.- The National Telecommunications Commission, in coordination with the Ministry of Infrastructure, shall establish as a priority for the purpose of progressively achieving the obligations of the Universal Service the following provisions: 3. That there is a sufficient supply of public pay telephones in the public domain, throughout the Venezuelan geographic space.

ARTICLE 50.- The National Telecommunications Commission, in coordination with the Ministry of Infrastructure, shall establish as a priority for the purpose of progressively achieving the obligations of the Universal Service the following provisions: 4. That all persons have access to the world wide web Internet.

ARTICLE 50.- The National Telecommunications Commission, in coordination with the Ministry of Infrastructure, shall establish as a priority for the purpose of progressively achieving the obligations of the Universal Service the following provisions: 5. That disabled users or those with special social needs have access to the public fixed telephone service available to the public, on conditions comparable to those offered to other users.

ARTICLE 52.- To guarantee the Universal Telecommunications Service throughout the Venezuelan geographic space, the assignment of Universal Service obligations shall be submitted, in each case, to open selection processes in which interested operators may participate, and the obligation shall be assigned to the interested operator that requires a smaller amount from the Universal Service Fund, provided that the technical requirements and quality levels established by the National Telecommunications Commission are met. The procedure for the assignment of Universal Service obligations shall be determined by regulation. The National Telecommunications Commission shall publish annually the list of geographic areas and services subject to Universal Service obligations, complying with the requirements provided for that purpose in the respective regulation.

ARTICLE 47.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall keep a register of its actions, which shall contain, at least, the following: 7. The models of telecommunications service contracts previously authorized by the National Telecommunications Commission.

ARTICLE 113.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall assign the numbering resource under objective, transparent and non-discriminatory conditions, in accordance with the regulations it establishes by resolution, following the provisions in the National Numbering Plans. Telecommunications service operators that provide services to the public shall have the right to dispose of numbers and numbering intervals when necessary to enable their effective provision, and shall comply with the National Numbering Plans.

ARTICLE 114.- The National Numbering Plans shall be issued by the National Telecommunications Commission respecting the international agreements subscribed and ratified by the Republic. Such plans shall be of mandatory observance, and therefore network operators, service providers, manufacturers and equipment suppliers must take the necessary measures for their compliance, as well as the decisions adopted in relation thereto by the National Telecommunications Commission. The modification of the content of the National Numbering Plans shall be aimed at procuring an efficient distribution of the resource or the fulfillment of the international obligations of the Republic. In any case, the National Telecommunications Commission shall take into account the interests of those affected and the adaptation costs required, in order to minimize them as much as possible and insofar as compatible with the causes that originated the modification.

ARTICLE 116.- In exercise of the functions that correspond to it as administrator and controller of the numbering resource, the National Telecommunications Commission may require from the holders of limited resources the information it deems necessary to evaluate the efficiency of the numbering systems and the adequate use of the resources assigned. The information collected shall be confidential and may only be used by the National Telecommunications Commission for the purposes requested.

ARTICLE 117.- Telecommunications operators shall guarantee, in the cases, terms, conditions and time periods determined by the National Telecommunications Commission, that contracting parties may conserve the numbers that have been assigned to them in accordance with the modalities that the National Telecommunications Commission establishes, based on international norms and trends. The conservation of numbering must not, in any case, worsen the availability and quality of the service. Without prejudice to the foregoing, it is established as a minimum obligation that telecommunications network operators must satisfy the conservation of telephone numbers of service subscribers when they decide to change operator or physical location within the same locality. To enjoy the numbering conservation established in this Law, service subscribers must be solvent with the operator that provides them with the service. The costs entailed by compliance with the obligations referred to in this article shall be for the exclusive account of the respective operators, without their being able to claim any compensation for such concept.

ARTICLE 118.- Telecommunications operators shall guarantee, in the cases, terms, conditions and time periods determined by the National Telecommunications Commission, that service subscribers may select, according to their convenience, among the telecommunications operators that provide national or international long distance telephony services, which of them to use, without this obligation worsening the availability and quality of the service.

ARTICLE 125.- The telecommunications service operators duly authorized by the National Telecommunications Commission under the terms established in this Law may operate with satellites owned by international entities established under the protection of treaties or international agreements signed and ratified by the Republic. Such entities are exempted from establishing legal personality in the country and from applying for the enabling title.

ARTICLE 126.- Any person who exclusively or predominantly owns or controls a general telecommunication route must permit the access or use of the same by telecommunication operators who request it, when its replacement is not feasible for physical, legal, economic, technical, environmental, security or operational reasons.

ARTICLE 126.- All operators shall have the right to make use of existing general telecommunication routes, in the manner and modalities determined by the National Telecommunications Commission, in accordance with this Law and other applicable legal provisions.

ARTICLE 127.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall endeavor to ensure the rational and non-discriminatory use of general telecommunication routes and shall also promote their creation and exploitation.

ARTICLE 128.- General telecommunication routes may be used by persons other than the one who owns or controls them, in which case such use shall generate the payment of a consideration to be fixed by mutual agreement between the parties.

ARTICLE 128.- A person who desires to make use of a general telecommunication route must request it in writing to the person who owns or controls it, indicating all the technical elements of the project to be developed and other requirements provided by the Regulation of this Law.

ARTICLE 128.- The applicant must send a copy of said request to the National Telecommunications Commission, within ten business days following, for its information.

ARTICLE 128.- Likewise, the parties shall send to the National Telecommunications Commission a copy of the agreement they reach, within ten business days following its signature, for its review.

ARTICLE 128.- The exercise of the right of access and use of a general telecommunication route must not irrationally affect the free use of the same by the person who owns or controls it, cause damage to that person's installations or affect the continuity and quality of their service.

ARTICLE 128.- The parties shall agree by mutual agreement the terms and conditions under which access to and use of general telecommunication routes will be carried out.

ARTICLE 129.- In the event that a party refuses to permit the access and use of a general telecommunication route or abstains from issuing a pronouncement thereon within the time established by the Regulation of this Law, the other party may request the National Telecommunications Commission that, after hearing the interested parties, it pronounce thereon, hearing the opinion of the Superintendency for the Promotion and Protection of Free Competition. In its decision the National Telecommunications Commission shall declare, if applicable, the irreplaceability of the general telecommunication route and consequently the forced execution of the obligation to permit access and use, under the terms and conditions fixed for that purpose.

ARTICLE 129.- The decision of the National Telecommunications Commission must be issued within a period not greater than thirty continuous days counted from the receipt of the request, extendable for an equal period if the complexity of the matter so warrants and is declared by express act.

ARTICLE 130.- Operators of telecommunications networks have the obligation to interconnect with other public telecommunications networks with the objective of establishing interoperable and continuous communications over time among the users of their services. Interconnection shall be made in accordance with the principles of neutrality, good faith, non-discrimination, and equal access among operators, pursuant to the terms established in this Law, its regulations and other applicable rules.

ARTICLE 131.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall prepare and administer the fundamental technical plans of numbering, transmission, signaling, charging and synchronization, among others, to which operators of telecommunications networks must adhere.

ARTICLE 132.- The initiative to request interconnection may come from any of the operators involved. In case of request, it must be made in writing, indicating with full precision the technical and economic elements applicable. The applicant must send a copy of the request with the corresponding acknowledgment of receipt to the National Telecommunications Commission, for its information.

ARTICLE 132.- In the interconnection agreement the parties shall provide with full precision the obligations to be borne by each operator.

ARTICLE 132.- In the interconnection agreement the parties shall provide with full precision the expression of the term in which either of them may request from the other the revision of the respective agreement. Said term may not exceed two years.

ARTICLE 133.- The parties shall set by mutual agreement the interconnection charges in the contracts that they execute for that purpose, orienting them to costs that include a reasonable profit margin.

ARTICLE 134.- Operators must notify the National Telecommunications Commission, within five business days following their execution, of the interconnection agreements to which they have arrived.

ARTICLE 134.- Within forty-five days following the notification, the National Telecommunications Commission shall make the comments it deems appropriate to the corresponding interconnection agreement, which shall have the character of an informative addendum to it and shall be available to the public together with the interconnection agreement.

ARTICLE 136.- The National Telecommunications Commission may order, as a precautionary measure or in its final decision, the disconnection from the networks of any of the parties when it deems it appropriate, in accordance with the applicable legal provisions.

ARTICLE 136.- The National Telecommunications Commission must prescribe the measures that will be applied with the purpose of minimizing negative effects for users, in case of disconnection.

ARTICLE 137.- The interconnection among telecommunications networks must be effected without impairing the services and quality originally provided, in such a way that it complies with the plans and programs in the field of telecommunications approved by the National Telecommunications Commission.

ARTICLE 137.- The responsibility for the service and its quality shall rest upon the operator contracted by the user, unless he demonstrates causes not imputable to him.

ARTICLE 139.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall authorize for the installation and operation of amateur radio stations persons of Venezuelan nationality, and foreigners resident in Venezuela or in transit in national territory, provided that they comply with the requirements established in this Law, its regulation and other rules issued on the matter.

ARTICLE 140.- Amateur radio stations may only be operated in the territory of the Republic by persons previously authorized by the National Telecommunications Commission.

ARTICLE 140.- The holder of an authorization must not use his equipment for purposes other than those for which the authorization was granted.

ARTICLE 140.- The holder of an authorization must not permit any person to operate his station without the corresponding authorization.

ARTICLE 141.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall keep a public register of the national or foreign entities recommended for the certification and homologation of telecommunications equipment.

ARTICLE 142.- The National Telecommunications Commission, through the certification entities, national or foreign, that it has recognized for such purposes, shall homologate and certify telecommunications equipment and apparatus manufactured or assembled in Venezuela.

ARTICLE 142.- The National Telecommunications Commission is the body responsible for supervising and requiring the homologation certificates or certification seals that telecommunications equipment must carry.

ARTICLE 143.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall issue the technical standards relating to the homologation of telecommunications equipment and apparatus and shall approve and publish a list of homologated brands and models and the uses that may be given to them. Inclusion on this list shall imply automatic compliance with the certification requirement, provided that the use is in accordance with that foreseen in the respective homologation.

ARTICLE 143.- The National Telecommunications Commission may require the homologation of certain equipment or installations not specifically intended to provide telecommunications services but which, by their nature, may cause interference to them.

ARTICLE 143.- In the cases referred to in this article, the list of homologated brands and models shall be permanently updated ex officio by the National Telecommunications Commission. Said body shall have the obligation to pronounce on the homologation of equipment and apparatus within the period set by the regulation of this Law, which shall not exceed ninety days.

ARTICLE 144.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall set the amount of the fees applicable in the process of obtaining homologation, with the purpose of absorbing the costs of the tests that must be carried out in the verification tasks.

ARTICLE 164.- A fine of up to five thousand Tax Units (5000 T.U.), in accordance with what the Regulation of this Law provides, shall be imposed for: 3. The use of service contracts whose basic models have not been approved by the National Telecommunications Commission.

ARTICLE 166.- A fine of up to fifty thousand Tax Units (50,000 T.U.), in accordance with what the Regulation of this Law provides, shall be imposed for: 1. The installation, operation and exploitation of telecommunications services or the use of radioelectric spectrum frequencies that require administrative authorization or concession without holding them.

ARTICLE 172.- The revocation of the administrative authorization or concession to natural or legal persons shall entail their disqualification for a period of five years to obtain another, directly or indirectly. Said period shall run from the moment the administrative act becomes definitively final.

ARTICLE 176.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall exercise its sanctioning power observing the principles of legality, impartiality, rationality and proportionality.

ARTICLE 177.- The procedures for the determination of the infringements referred to in this Title shall be initiated by complaint, ex officio, or by initiative of the National Telecommunications Commission.

ARTICLE 179.- The act of opening the sanctioning procedure shall be issued by the Director General of the National Telecommunications Commission, after hearing the opinion of the Legal Counsel of the body, and shall establish with clarity the facts imputed and the consequences that could derive from the finding thereof, summoning the alleged infringer so that within a period not greater than fifteen business days he files the allegations and evidence he deems pertinent for his defense.

ARTICLE 181.- In the substantiation of the administrative sanctioning procedure the National Telecommunications Commission shall have the broadest investigative powers, governing its activity by the principle of freedom of evidence. Among other acts, within its substantiation activity the National Telecommunications Commission may: 1. Summon any person to testify in relation to the alleged infringement; 2. Require persons related to the procedure to provide documents or information pertinent to clarify the facts; 3. Call, through the national or regional press, any interested person who could provide information related to the alleged infringement; 4. Request from other public bodies relevant information regarding the persons involved, provided that the information they have has not been declared confidential or secret in accordance with the law; 5. Carry out the inspections it deems pertinent for the investigation; 6. Collect the necessary evidence to clarify the facts subject to the sanctioning procedure.

ARTICLE 182.- In the course of administrative sanctioning procedures the National Telecommunications Commission may prescribe the precautionary measures referred to in this Section, for which purpose it must perform a weighing between the serious damages that operators and users affected by the conduct of the alleged infringer could suffer, and the damages that the adoption of such measure would imply for him, all in attention to the presumption of good right emerging from the situation.

ARTICLE 183.- The precautionary measures that the National Telecommunications Commission may adopt, attending to the parameters established in the previous article, may consist of: 1. Ordering the immediate suspension, total or partial, of the activities allegedly infringing this Law; 2. Ordering the carrying out of acts or actions in matters of Universal Service, interconnection, right of way, restoration of services, billing of services, security and defense; 3. Proceeding to the seizure of the equipment employed and closure of the premises or establishments where operation takes place, when dealing with allegedly clandestine activities that imply the use of the radioelectric spectrum.

ARTICLE 184.- Once the precautionary measure has been ordered, the party against whom it operates or any interested party may oppose it within five business days following the notification thereof to the party against whom the measure operates. In case of opposition, a probative articulation period of eight business days shall be opened, in which the parties and the interested parties may present their evidence and allegations. Once this period has expired, the National Telecommunications Commission shall decide what is appropriate within the following three business days.

ARTICLE 185.- Once the substantiation of the file is concluded or the period for it has elapsed, it shall be referred to the Director General of the National Telecommunications Commission who, without prejudice to ordering any additional substantiation act he deems convenient, must issue the corresponding decision within fifteen continuous days following its receipt. This period may be extended by reasoned order for up to fifteen continuous days when the complexity of the case so requires.

ARTICLE 186.- In the decision of the Director General the existence or not of infringements shall be determined and, if affirmative, the corresponding sanctions shall be established, as well as the corrective measures applicable, except in cases of revocation, whose decision corresponds to the Board of Directors or the Minister of Infrastructure in accordance with this Law.

ARTICLE 187.- The person sanctioned by the decision of the National Telecommunications Commission must voluntarily execute what is ordered in the respective act within the period set by said provision. If the individual does not voluntarily execute the decision of the National Telecommunications Commission, it may enforce it forcibly in accordance with the provisions of the Organic Law of Administrative Procedures, unless by express legal decision it must be entrusted to a judicial authority.

ARTICLE 192.- Notwithstanding legal provisions on security and defense, the President of the Republic may, directly or through the National Telecommunications Commission, order operators that provide subscription television services, through their customer information channel, and radio broadcasting and open television companies, the free transmission of official messages or addresses from the Presidency or Vice-Presidency of the Republic or from the Ministers. By regulation the modalities, limitations and other characteristics of such emissions and transmissions shall be determined.

ARTICLE 195.- The signing of a merger agreement between telecommunications operating companies, the total or partial acquisition of these companies by other operating companies, as well as their spin-off, transformation or the creation of subsidiaries that exploit telecommunications services, when they imply a change in control over them, must be submitted to the approval of the National Telecommunications Commission so that such operations acquire effectiveness. For these purposes, the interested parties shall send to the National Telecommunications Commission a copy of the corresponding documents within ten days following the realization of the operation.

ARTICLE 195.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall only approve the operations referred to in this article when there is a favorable opinion from the Superintendency for the Promotion and Protection of Free Competition.

ARTICLE 195.- The administrative act by which the realization of the operation is not approved must clearly state its grounds and, if applicable, make the pertinent recommendations. The act of rejection shall definitively prevent the execution of the operation as planned, unless the interested parties adopt the observations or recommendations made by the Superintendency for the Promotion and Protection of Free Competition, or the act of rejection is annulled by a final and definitive decision.

ARTICLE 196.- Whoever requests from the National Telecommunications Commission the incorporation as concrete attributes of his administrative authorization of certain provisions to offer them to the public must state in the respective project, under oath, whether any company linked to him provides the same service or similar services. In such cases the National Telecommunications Commission shall request the opinion of the Superintendency for the Promotion and Protection of Free Competition on the effects that granting the requested attribute could have on the market, after hearing the interested parties.

ARTICLE 196.- The National Telecommunications Commission, without prejudice to its other powers, shall abstain from approving the respective project if the opinion of the Superintendency is unfavorable.

ARTICLE 200.- The State shall promote the existence of community radio broadcasting and open television stations of public service, non-profit, as means for communication and plural and transparent action of communities organized within their respective scope. Their regime, regulation, characteristics, requirements and limitations shall be determined by regulation, in accordance with the National Telecommunications Plan and the National Table of Frequency Band Allocation (CUNABAF).

ARTICLE 202.- The State, through the National Telecommunications Commission, shall promote the use and updating of technological innovations in all their modalities, with the purpose that permanent technological modernization plans be established in the field of telecommunications.

ARTICLE 208.- Until the law that regulates the content of transmissions and communications carried out through the different means of telecommunication is enacted, the National Executive, by regulation, may continue establishing the regulations it deems necessary. All existing legal and regulatory provisions and any other regulatory instrument that regulate, limit or restrict the content of such transmissions or communications shall remain in force, except as provided by the National Assembly or the National Executive, as the case may be, and in particular those contained in the listed decrees and regulations.

ARTICLE 208.- The National Telecommunications Commission shall continue to be in charge of ensuring faithful compliance with the regulation referred to in this article and that issued by the National Executive in this matter until the law regulating the content of transmissions and communications is enacted.

ARTICLE 209.- Until the corresponding law is enacted, the National Executive may, when it considers it convenient to the interests of the Nation, or when public order or security so requires, suspend the transmission of communications carried out through the different means of telecommunications, all in accordance with the Constitution of the Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela.

ARTICLE 211.- The National Executive, through the Regulation for the Opening of the Basic Telephony Service, shall determine the model, conditions, limitations, requirements and any other aspect necessary to obtain the conditions it deems convenient for the opening of the Basic Telephony service, for which purpose it may establish asymmetric regulations.

ARTICLE 221.- The current basic telephony operator may only provide the subscription television service starting on November 28, 2000. Between November 28, 2000 and November 28, 2002, the current basic telephony concessionaire may provide the subscription television service provided that it concurrently complies with the following conditions: 1. That it has acquired the corresponding attribute; 2. That it guarantees, to the satisfaction of the National Telecommunications Commission, the access of subscription television operators to ducts, manholes and other elements necessary for the placement of cables that are under its control or possession by any title.

ARTICLE 222.- No company other than the current basic telephony concessionaire may provide said service before November 28, 2000, the day following the cessation of the limited concurrence existing in the concession contract. From the publication of this Law in the Official Gazette and until November 28, 2002, no telephone operator company or companies linked to them, existing in the country before the entry into force of this Law, may directly or indirectly acquire, control or merge with subscription television operator companies existing in the country before the entry into force of this Law and vice versa. Likewise, during said period such companies may not among themselves constitute consortia, joint ventures or any other form of association for the provision of said services."
