(Venezuela 2000)_01,C_EXPRESS_ORDER,True,1.0,True,1.0,"Article 132.- In the same penalty shall incur the Venezuelan who solicits, manages or obtains, in any manner, the intervention of a foreign Government to overthrow the Venezuelan government; or who, before its officials, or by publications made in the foreign press, incites civil war in the Republic or defames its President or insults the diplomatic Representative or the consular officials of Venezuela, by reason of their functions, in the country where the act is committed.

Article 134.- Whoever, improperly and to the detriment of the Republic, has revealed the political or military secrets concerning the security of Venezuela, either by communicating or by publishing documents, data, drawings, plans or other information relating to material, fortifications or military operations, or by otherwise making their knowledge public, shall be punished with imprisonment of seven to ten years.

Article 134.- The penalty shall be eight to twelve years if the secrets have been revealed to a Nation that is at war with Venezuela or to the Agents of that Nation, or also if the act has caused the disturbance of the friendly relations of the Republic with another government.

Article 135.- Whoever has obtained the revelation of the secrets or has procured them by any illegitimate means, shall be punished with the penalties established in the preceding article, and in accordance with the distinctions made therein.

Article 136.- If the secrets specified in Article 134 have been divulged due to the negligence or imprudence of those who, by reason of their employment, were in possession of the drawings, plans or documents, or had knowledge of the secrets, the guilty parties shall be punished with imprisonment of forty-five days to nine months.

Article 141.- Whoever, out of contempt, seizes, breaks or destroys in a public place or open to the public, the national flag or another emblem of the Republic, shall be punished with imprisonment of two months to one year. If this crime is committed while the Republic is engaged in a foreign war, the imprisonment shall be thirteen months to two years.

Article 148.- Whoever offends by word or in writing, or in any other manner disrespects the President of the Republic or whoever is acting in his stead, shall be punished with imprisonment of six to thirty months, if the offense is serious, and with half of that penalty if it is minor.

Article 148, Sole Paragraph.- If the offense is against the President of one of the Legislative Chambers or the President of the Supreme Tribunal of Justice, the penalty shall be four months to two years when the offense is serious and half of that penalty when it is minor.

Article 149.- When the acts specified in the preceding article are carried out against the Governor of one of the States, or against the Ministers of the Cabinet, Executive Vice President of the Republic, Mayor of the Metropolitan District of Caracas, Magistrates of the Supreme Tribunal of Justice, Presidents of the Legislative Councils of the States and the Superior Judges, or against the person acting in their stead, the penalty indicated in said article shall be reduced to one half, and to one third if it concerns Mayors of Municipalities.

Article 150.- Whoever publicly vilifies the National Assembly, the Supreme Tribunal of Justice or the Cabinet or Council of Ministers, as well as any of the Legislative Councils of the States or any of the Superior Courts, shall be punished with imprisonment of fifteen days to ten months.

Article 152.- Prosecution for the acts referred to in the preceding articles shall not proceed except upon request of the person or body offended, made through the Representative of the Public Ministry, before the competent Judge.

Article 158.- Whoever commits a crime in the geographic space of the Republic against the Chief or First Magistrate of a foreign Nation shall incur the penalty applicable to the act committed with an increase of one sixth to one third.

Article 158.- If it were an act against the life, security or individual liberty of said person, the aggravation of the penalty, in accordance with the foregoing provision, may not be less than three years of imprisonment.

Article 159.- Whoever, by an act of contempt toward a foreign Power, seizes, breaks or destroys the flag or any other emblem of that Nation, shall be punished with arrest in a Fortress or Political Prison for a time of one to six months. Prosecution shall not proceed except at the instance of the foreign Government.

Article 160.- In cases of crimes committed in the geographic space of the Republic against the Representatives of foreign Powers accredited to the government of Venezuela, by reason of their functions, the penalties established for the same acts committed against Venezuelan public officials, by reason of their functions, shall be applied. If it is a matter of offenses, prosecution may not proceed except by the corresponding instance of the aggrieved party.

Article 186.- Whoever improperly opens any letter, telegram or closed package not addressed to him, or who improperly takes it to know its content, even if it is not closed, belonging to another, shall be punished with arrest of eight to twenty days.

Article 187.- Whoever has improperly suppressed any epistolary or telegraphic correspondence that does not belong to him, even though being closed he has not opened it, shall be punished with arrest of one to six months.

Article 188.- Whoever, having in his possession epistolary or telegraphic correspondence not intended for publicity, makes it improperly public, even if it has been addressed to him, provided that the act may cause some prejudice, shall be punished with a fine of fifty to one thousand bolívares.

Article 189.- Whoever, being employed in the postal or telegraph service, by abuse of his office appropriates any letter, telegram, communication or any other correspondence not open, or, if closed, opens it to know its content, or retains it or reveals its existence or content to a person other than the title of its destination, shall be punished with imprisonment of fifteen days to fifteen months.

Article 189.- The same penalty shall be imposed on whoever, in service and by abuse of the mentioned offices, suppresses any of said correspondences.

Article 190.- Whoever, by reason of his status, functions, profession, art or trade, has knowledge of some secret whose divulgation may cause some prejudice, nevertheless reveals it without just cause, shall be punished with imprisonment of five to thirty days.

Article 191.- With respect to the crimes provided in Articles 186, 187, 188 and 190, whenever the act has not caused some prejudice that affects public order, prosecution shall not proceed except upon accusation of the aggrieved party.

Article 200.- Whoever, without obtaining his object, engages in persuading or inducing any public official to commit any of the crimes provided in the preceding articles shall be punished, when the inducement is with the object that the official incur in the crime provided by Article 198, with a fine of one hundred fifty to one thousand bolívares; and if it is with the purpose that he incur in that indicated by Article 199, with the penalties established there, but reduced by one half.

Article 204.- Whoever is a public official who, in the exercise of his functions, incites any person to disobey the laws or measures taken by the authority shall be punished with the same penalty established for ordering or executing arbitrary acts (imprisonment of fifteen days to one year, increased by one sixth if done for private interest).

Article 206.- Any public official who communicates or publishes the documents or facts of which he is aware or in possession by reason of his functions and which he must keep secret shall be punished with arrest of three to fifteen months, and likewise any public official who in any way favours the dissemination of those documents or facts shall be punished.

Article 208.- Any public official who, having acquired in the exercise of his functions knowledge resulting from those same functions of some punishable fact for which the law orders that proceedings be undertaken ex officio, omits or unduly delays notifying the competent authority of it shall be punished with a fine of fifty to one thousand bolívares.

Article 210.- The minister of any cult who, in the exercise of his functions, treats the institutions, the laws of the Republic or the acts of the authority with public contempt or vilification shall be punished with arrest of one to six months.

Article 211.- The minister of any cult who, taking advantage of his character, excites contempt and disobedience of the institutions, laws or provisions of the authority, or of the duties inherent to a public office, shall be punished with arrest in Fortress or Political Prison for a time of forty-five days to one year.

Article 211.- The minister of any cult who, taking advantage of his character, constrains, induces or persuades any person to carry out acts or to make declarations contrary to the laws or to the rights acquired by virtue thereof shall be punished with the same penalties.

Article 223.- Whoever, by word or deed, in any way offends the honor, reputation or decorum of a member of the National Assembly or of some public official shall be punished as follows, if the act took place in his presence and by reason of his functions: (1) If the offense was directed against an agent of the public force, with imprisonment of one to three months.

Article 223.- Whoever, by word or deed, in any way offends the honor, reputation or decorum of a member of the National Assembly or of some public official shall be punished as follows, if the act took place in his presence and by reason of his functions: (2) If the offense was directed against a member of the National Assembly or some public official, with imprisonment of one month to one year, according to the category of those persons.

Article 224.- Whoever in any other way and outside the cases provided in the previous Chapter uses violence or threat against a member of the National Assembly or any public official, if the act takes place by reason of the functions of the offended person, shall be punished with the same penalties.

Article 226.- Whoever, by word or deed, offends in any way the honor, reputation, decorum or dignity of any judicial, political or administrative body, if the crime has been committed in the act of being constituted, or of some magistrate in audience, shall be punished with imprisonment of three months to two years.

Article 226.- The prosecution shall not take place except upon request of the offended body. If the crime has been committed against bodies not assembled, the prosecution shall only take place upon request of the members who preside over them.

Article 227.- In the cases provided in the preceding articles, no proof at all about the truth, not even about the notoriety of the facts or of the defects imputed to the offended party, shall be admitted from the guilty party.

Article 229.- In all other cases not provided for by a special provision of the law, whoever commits some crime against a member of the National Assembly or any public official by reason of his functions shall incur the penalty established for the committed crime, plus an increase of one sixth to one third.

Article 241.- Whoever, knowingly that an individual is innocent, denounces or accuses him before the judicial authority, or before a public official who has the obligation to transmit the denunciation or complaint, attributing to him a punishable fact, or simulating the appearances or material signs of a punishable fact, shall incur the penalty of six to thirty months of imprisonment.

Article 242.- The penalties established in the preceding article shall be reduced to two thirds if the guilty party of the specified offense has retracted his imputations or has revealed the simulation before any act of prosecution against the calumniated person.

Article 242.- The said penalties shall only be reduced to one half if the retraction or revelation intervenes before the sentence that is rendered on account of the false accusation.

Article 243.- Whoever, deposing as a witness before the judicial authority, affirms the false or denies the true or keeps silent, totally or partially, what he knows in relation to the facts about which he is interrogated shall be punished with imprisonment of fifteen days to fifteen months.

Article 243.- If the false testimony has been given against some accused for a crime or in the course of a criminal trial, the imprisonment shall be six to thirty months, and if both circumstances concur, it shall be eighteen months to three years.

Article 243.- If the false testimony has been the cause of a condemnatory sentence to a presidio penalty, the imprisonment shall be three to five years.

Article 243.- If the testimony has been given without oath, the penalty shall be reduced by one sixth to one third.

Article 244.- The following shall be exempt from all penalty for the offense provided in the preceding article: (1) The witness who, if he had spoken the truth, would have inevitably exposed his own person, that of a close relative, close friend or benefactor to a grave danger touching freedom or honor.

Article 245.- Whoever, having declared in the course of a criminal proceeding, retracts his false testimony and testifies according to the truth before the conclusion of the summary inquiry by order of no ground to continue it or the process by order of dismissal founded on lack of merits for charges or before the falsity of the testimony is discovered, shall be exempt from all penalty in relation to the offense provided in Article 243.

Article 247.- (2) In the cases provided in the first paragraph of said article, with imprisonment of one to three years, or of two to four years, respectively, if the two indicated circumstances concur.

Article 247.- (3) In the case of the second paragraph of the same article, with imprisonment of four to five years.

Article 247.- If the false testimony, expert report or interpretation have been made without oath the penalty shall be reduced by one sixth to one third.

Article 247.- All that which the briber shall have given shall be confiscated.

Article 284.- (2) If it is an offense whose penalty is imprisonment, with imprisonment of three to twelve months.

Article 284.- (3) In all other cases, with a fine of fifty to one thousand bolívares, according to the magnitude of the instigated fact.

Article 285.- In the cases of numbers 2 and 3 of the previous article, the penalty may never exceed one third of the penalty fixed for the punishable fact to which the instigation refers.

Article 286.- Whoever publicly excites disobedience of the Laws or hatred of some inhabitants against others or makes the apology of an act that the law foresees as a crime, in such manner that public tranquility is put in danger, shall be punished with imprisonment of forty-five days to six months.

Article 335.- Whoever, by propagating false news or by other fraudulent means, has produced in the markets or in the stock exchanges some increase or decrease in the price of wages, goods, merchandise, fruits or negotiable securities admitted in those places or in stock quotation lists, shall be punished with imprisonment of three to fifteen months. If the crime has been committed by brokers or public exchange agents, the penalty shall be imprisonment of six to thirty months.

Article 373.- Whoever, by propagating false news or using other fraudulent means, has produced scarcity and an increase in the price of foodstuffs shall be punished with imprisonment of six to thirty months. If the guilty party is some public broker, said penalty shall be increased by one half.

Article 444.- Whoever, communicating with several persons gathered or separate, shall have imputed to some individual a specific fact capable of exposing him to public contempt or hatred, or offensive to his honor or reputation, shall be punished with imprisonment of three to eighteen months.

Article 444.- If the crime is committed in a public document or with writings, drawings disseminated or exhibited to the public, or with other means of publicity, the penalty shall be imprisonment of six to thirty months.

Article 445.- The individual charged with the crime of defamation shall not be permitted to offer proof of the truth or notoriety of the defamatory fact, except in the following cases: 1.- When the offended person is a public official and provided that the fact imputed to him relates to the exercise of his ministry; however, without prejudice to the provisions of Articles 223 and 227.

Article 445.- The individual charged with the crime of defamation shall not be permitted to offer proof of the truth or notoriety of the defamatory fact, except in the following cases: 2.- When by the imputed fact a trial is initiated or there is a pending trial against the defamed person.

Article 445.- The individual charged with the crime of defamation shall not be permitted to offer proof of the truth or notoriety of the defamatory fact, except in the following cases: 3.- When the complainant formally requests that in the judgment there also be a pronouncement on the truth or falsity of the defamatory fact.

Article 445.- If the truth of the fact is proven or if the defamed person is convicted, by reason of the defamation, for the fact, the author of the defamation shall be exempt from the penalty, except in the case that the means employed constituted in themselves the crime provided in the following article.

Article 446.- Any individual who, in communication with several persons, together or separate, shall have offended in any manner the honor, reputation or decorum of some person, shall be punished with arrest of three to eight days, or fine of twenty-five to one hundred fifty bolívares.

Article 446.- Any individual who, in communication with several persons, together or separate, shall have offended in any manner the honor, reputation or decorum of some person, if the act is committed in the presence of the offended, even if he is alone, or by means of some writing addressed to him or in a public place, may be punished with imprisonment of up to thirty days or five hundred bolívares fine, and if publicity concurs with the presence of the offended, the penalty may be increased to forty-five days imprisonment or six hundred bolívares fine.

Article 446.- Any individual who, in communication with several persons, together or separate, shall have offended in any manner the honor, reputation or decorum of some person, if the act is committed using the means indicated in the aside of Article 444, the imprisonment penalty shall be for a time of fifteen days to three months, or fine of one hundred fifty to one thousand five hundred bolívares.

Article 447.- When the crime provided in the preceding article has been committed against some person legitimately charged with a public service, in his presence and by reason of said service, the guilty party shall be punished with arrest of fifteen to forty-five days. If there is publicity, the imprisonment may be imposed of one to two months.

Article 448.- When, in the cases provided in the two preceding articles, the offended party has been the determining and unjust cause of the act, the penalty shall be reduced in the proportion of one to two thirds.

Article 448.- When, in the cases provided in the two preceding articles, the offenses were reciprocal, the Judge may, according to the circumstances, declare the parties or some of them exempt from any penalty.

Article 450.- At the request of the complainant, the condemnatory sentence shall be published at the expense of the convicted party, once or twice, in the newspapers that the Judge shall indicate.

Article 451.- The crimes provided in the present Chapter may not be prosecuted except by accusation of the aggrieved party or of their legal representatives.

Article 451.- In the case of an offense against any Judicial, political or administrative Body, or against representatives of said Body, prosecution shall not take place except by authorization of the Body or of its hierarchical chief, if it is not constituted as a college or corporation; in these cases, procedure shall be as ordered in Article 226.

Article 452.- The criminal action for the prosecution of the crimes provided in the present Chapter shall prescribe in one year in the cases referred to in Article 444, and in three months in the cases specified in Articles 446 and 447.

Article 494.- Whoever, without permission of the authority when such permission is required by law, shall have put on sale or distributed in a public place printed materials, drawings or manuscripts, shall be punished with a fine of fifty bolívares as maximum.

Article 494.- If the printed materials or drawings have already been seized by the authority, the penalty shall be arrest up to thirty days and a fine of fifty to one hundred fifty bolívares.

Article 495.- If the news are false or supposed, the penalty shall be a fine of fifty to one hundred fifty bolívares or arrest up to fifteen days.

Article 497.- Whoever in any way shall have torn down, destroyed or by any other means rendered unusable the printed materials, drawings or manuscripts that the authority has had fixed, shall be punished with a fine of twenty to one hundred bolívares; and if he does it with contempt of the authority, he shall be punished with arrest up to fifteen days.

Article 497.- If the printed materials, drawings or manuscripts have been fixed by private individuals observing for this purpose the provisions of the law or of the authority, and the act has been executed the same day or the following day after the fixation, the penalty shall be a fine not exceeding fifty bolívares."
