(Zambia 2025)_01,C_EXPRESS_ORDER,False,,True,1.0,"3. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority or in excess of authority, infringe any security measure, access or monitor a computer system or any part of a computer system of another person.

3. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

4. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority, interfere with data or a computer system of another person in a way which— (a) causes the data or computer system to be modified, destroyed, altered or otherwise rendered ineffective; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

4. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority, interfere with data or a computer system of another person in a way which— (b) obstructs, interrupts or interferes with another person’s lawful use of data; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

4. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority, interfere with data or a computer system of another person in a way which— (c) denies access to data to a person who is authorised to access it; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

4. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority, interfere with data or a computer system of another person in a way which— (d) causes data of another person to deteriorate; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

4. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority, interfere with data or a computer system of another person in a way which— (e) deletes data of another person. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

5. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not less than seven years and not exceeding fifteen years.

6. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not less than seven years and not exceeding fifteen years.

7. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding one million penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding ten years, or to both.

10. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding two hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding two years, or to both.

10. (3) Despite subsection (1), it shall not be an offence to record a private conversation where— (a) the private conversation is unintentionally recorded through a device;

12. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority, input, alter, delete, or suppress computer data resulting in inauthentic data, with the intent that the inauthentic data be considered or acted on by another person as if it were authentic, regardless of whether the data is directly readable or intelligible.

12. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

12. (3) A person commits an offence if that person with intent to defraud or deceive another person for the purpose of procuring an economic benefit for oneself or another person— (a) inputs, alters, deletes or suppresses electronic data; (4) A person who is convicted of an offence under subsection (3), is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

12. (3) A person commits an offence if that person with intent to defraud or deceive another person for the purpose of procuring an economic benefit for oneself or another person— (b) interferes with the functioning of a computer or computer system. (4) A person who is convicted of an offence under subsection (3), is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

13. (1) A person commits an offence if that person, through a computer system with intent to extort or gain anything from any person— (b) threatens that any person shall be accused by any other person of commission of an offence; (2) A person convicted of an offence under subsection (1), is liable to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units or imprisonment for a period not exceeding seven years, or to both.

13. (1) A person commits an offence if that person, through a computer system with intent to extort or gain anything from any person— (c) knowing the contents of the writing, causes any person to receive any writing containing such accusation or threat; (2) A person convicted of an offence under subsection (1), is liable to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units or imprisonment for a period not exceeding seven years, or to both.

13. (1) A person commits an offence if that person, through a computer system with intent to extort or gain anything from any person— (d) knowingly transmits any communication containing any threat to cause damage to a computer system with the intent to extort from any person any money or other thing of value; (2) A person convicted of an offence under subsection (1), is liable to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units or imprisonment for a period not exceeding seven years, or to both.

13. (1) A person commits an offence if that person, through a computer system with intent to extort or gain anything from any person— (f) compels another person to perform or to abstain from performing any act; (2) A person convicted of an offence under subsection (1), is liable to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units or imprisonment for a period not exceeding seven years, or to both.

15. (1) A person commits an offence if that person intentionally— (a) produces child pornography for the purpose of its distribution through a computer or computer system; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

15. (1) A person commits an offence if that person intentionally— (b) sells, imports, exports or makes available any pornography to a child through a computer or computer system; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

15. (1) A person commits an offence if that person intentionally— (c) compels, invites or allows a child to view pornography through a computer or computer system; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

15. (1) A person commits an offence if that person intentionally— (d) offers or makes available child pornography through a computer or computer system; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

15. (1) A person commits an offence if that person intentionally— (e) distributes or transmits child pornography through a computer or computer system; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

15. (1) A person commits an offence if that person intentionally— (g) possesses child pornography in a computer or computer system or on a computer data storage medium; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

15. (1) A person commits an offence if that person intentionally— (h) obtains access of child pornography through a computer or computer system. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

16. (1) A person shall not intentionally, using a computer or computer system— (a) arrange a meeting with a child with the intent of abusing or engaging in sexual activity with the child or producing child pornography, whether or not that person takes any steps to effect such a meeting; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding twenty-five years.

17. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for a term of not less than fifteen years and not exceeding twenty-five years.

18. (1) A person shall not, using a computer or computer system, intentionally engage in trafficking in persons.

19. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority— (a) use a computer or computer system to relay or retransmit electronic communications to deceive or mislead users of a computer or computer system as to the origin of such communication; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units, or imprisonment for a term of not less than two years and not exceeding seven years, or to both.

19. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority— (b) initiate the transmission of multiple electronic messages from or through a computer or computer system for purposes of causing harm or disrupting the computer or computer system; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units, or imprisonment for a term of not less than two years and not exceeding seven years, or to both.

19. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority— (c) establish a software application system with the intent to deceive or mislead a visitor to the software application system as to the authenticity of the software application system for the purpose of gaining unauthorised access to information to commit an offence; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units, or imprisonment for a term of not less than two years and not exceeding seven years, or to both.

19. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority— (d) falsify header information in an electronic communication and initiate the transmission of such electronic communication to commit an offence, deceive or mislead users. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding seven hundred thousand penalty units, or imprisonment for a term of not less than two years and not exceeding seven years, or to both.

21. (1) A person who receives an order relating to a criminal investigation under this Act, shall not without lawful excuse, disclose— (a) that an order has been made; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

21. (1) A person who receives an order relating to a criminal investigation under this Act, shall not without lawful excuse, disclose— (b) anything done under the order; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

21. (1) A person who receives an order relating to a criminal investigation under this Act, shall not without lawful excuse, disclose— (c) any data collected or recorded under the order. (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding five hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.

22. (1) A person shall not use a computer or computer system to— (a) publish or transmit electronic data that is obscene, vulgar, lewd, lascivious or indecent with intent to humiliate, harass or cause substantial emotional distress to another person ; (3) A person who contravenes subsection (1) or (2), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding two hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding two years, or to both.

22. (1) A person shall not use a computer or computer system to— (b) repeatedly send to another person electronic data that is obscene, vulgar, lewd, lascivious or indecent with intent to humiliate or harass the other person to the detriment of that person’s health, emotional well-being, self-esteem or reputation. (3) A person who contravenes subsection (1) or (2), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding two hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding two years, or to both.

22. (2) A person shall not use a computer or computer system to disseminate information, statement or image, knowing the same to be false that— (a) causes damage to the reputation of another person; (3) A person who contravenes subsection (1) or (2), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding two hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding two years, or to both.

22. (2) A person shall not use a computer or computer system to disseminate information, statement or image, knowing the same to be false that— (b) subjects another person to public ridicule, contempt, hatred or embarrassment. (3) A person who contravenes subsection (1) or (2), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to a fine not exceeding two hundred thousand penalty units or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding two years, or to both.

24. (1) A person commits an offence if that person uses a computer or computer system to— (b) incite or attempt to incite ethnic divisions among the people of the Republic; (2) A person who contravenes subsection (1), commits an offence and is liable, on conviction, to imprisonment for life."
