(Zambia 2025)_01,GOV_WARRANT,False,,True,1.0,"5. (1) A person shall not intentionally and without lawful authority, communicate, disclose or transmit data, information, a programme, an access code or a command relating to critical information or critical information infrastructure to any person not authorised to access such data, information, programme, access code or command.

10. (3) Despite subsection (1), it shall not be an offence to record a private conversation where— (b) a law enforcement officer reasonably suspects that there is— (i) a threat to life or an imminent threat of serious violence to a person; (ii) a threat of substantial damage to property; or (iii) an offence under any written law that may be committed;

29. A person who has knowledge about the functioning of a computer or computer system or measures applied to protect the computer data that is the subject of a search under this Act may, permit and assist where reasonably required and requested by a person authorised to make the search by— (a) providing information that enables the undertaking of necessary measures in the circumstances;

29. A person who has knowledge about the functioning of a computer or computer system or measures applied to protect the computer data that is the subject of a search under this Act may, permit and assist where reasonably required and requested by a person authorised to make the search by— (b) accessing and using a computer system or computer data storage medium to search any computer data available to or in the computer system;

29. A person who has knowledge about the functioning of a computer or computer system or measures applied to protect the computer data that is the subject of a search under this Act may, permit and assist where reasonably required and requested by a person authorised to make the search by— (c) obtaining and copying such computer data;

29. A person who has knowledge about the functioning of a computer or computer system or measures applied to protect the computer data that is the subject of a search under this Act may, permit and assist where reasonably required and requested by a person authorised to make the search by— (d) obtaining an intelligible output from a computer system in a format that is admissible for the purpose of legal proceedings.

30. (1) A law enforcement officer may, where the law enforcement officer has grounds to believe that computer data reasonably required for the purposes of a criminal investigation is particularly vulnerable to loss or modification, by written notice given to a person in control of the computer data, require the person to ensure that the computer data specified in the notice be preserved for a period of up to seven days.

31. A law enforcement officer may, with a warrant, where the law enforcement officer is satisfied that computer data is reasonably required for the purposes of a criminal investigation, by written notice given to a person in control of the computer or computer system, require the person to disclose relevant traffic data about a specified communication to identify— (a) an electronic communications service provider;

31. A law enforcement officer may, with a warrant, where the law enforcement officer is satisfied that computer data is reasonably required for the purposes of a criminal investigation, by written notice given to a person in control of the computer or computer system, require the person to disclose relevant traffic data about a specified communication to identify— (b) a path through which a communication was transmitted.

32. (1) Where a judge is satisfied on the basis of an ex-parte application by a law enforcement officer, that there are reasonable grounds to suspect or believe that traffic data associated with a specified communication is reasonably required for the purposes of a criminal investigation, the judge may order a person in control of such data to— (a) collect or record traffic data associated with a specified communication during a specified period;

32. (1) Where a judge is satisfied on the basis of an ex-parte application by a law enforcement officer, that there are reasonable grounds to suspect or believe that traffic data associated with a specified communication is reasonably required for the purposes of a criminal investigation, the judge may order a person in control of such data to— (b) permit and assist a specified law enforcement officer to collect or record that data.

32. (2) The judge may, where the judge is satisfied on the basis of an ex-parte application by a law enforcement officer that there are reasonable grounds to suspect or believe that traffic data is reasonably required for the purposes of a criminal investigation, authorise a law enforcement officer to collect or record traffic data associated with a specified communication during a specified period."
