(El Salvador 1997)_02,GOV_OVERSIGHT,False,,True,1.0,"Art. 115-A.- The concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right of exploitation of concessioned frequencies. In any total or partial transfer of the spectrum the acquirer shall pay SIGET the administrative and registration processing costs according to the tariff structure of costs in force in the Registry published by SIGET, which will be indexed each year with the CPI.

Art. 116-A.- The calculation of the Annual Rate charge is directly related to the number of subscribers to whom the licensee provides the services in a given system, according to the license granted, such that as the diversification of convergent services is achieved, payments shall be annual and payable in advance during the months of January and February of each year and is determined by the following formula: TA = Fe * # subscribers, where TA = Annual Rate for broadcasting services terrestrial and satellite by subscription; Fe = Economic factor per year/subscriber, being USD$1.80 per subscriber; # = The number of subscribers reported on October 31 of each year to SIGET. The licensees of sound broadcasting and subscription television must present a sworn statement of the number of subscribers. The subscribers of the service packages reported by the licensees will be included within the subscriber portfolio, only if the package includes the subscription television service in any of its modalities. The values will be indexed annually according to the official CPI factor. The formula shall be reviewed by SIGET according to the methodology developed in the Regulation of this Law, and updated by means of an instruction at least every 3 years in accordance with technological advances. Any operator of subscription television services that provides coverage nationwide must incorporate in its programming grid free of charge and without any condition, the channels that operate in the frequencies of free reception television broadcasting services with national coverage, whether this spectrum is of regulated use or official use. The operator of subscription television services that provides nationwide coverage is released from the obligation established in the preceding paragraph when, in its bilateral relationship, any operator of frequencies of free reception television broadcasting services establishes a price for the inclusion of its channel in the programming grid. If there is no agreement on the price between the aforementioned parties, the subscription television service operator may not transmit the respective channel in its grid. The channels of official and regulated use spectrum with national coverage will maintain their same location in the programming grid; this provision applies to terrestrial and satellite subscription television service operators. In the case of regional and local free reception television channels, their incorporation into the programming grid will be by agreement between the parties and according to the area of operation of each system. SIGET shall guarantee compliance with the provisions of this Article."
(El Salvador 1997)_02,GOV_OVERSIGHT_2_1,False,,False,,
(El Salvador 1997)_02,GOV_REGULATOR,False,,True,1.0,"Art. 1.- It is also established that the Superintendencia General de Electricidad y Telecomunicaciones (SIGET) shall be the entity responsible for applying and ensuring compliance with the norms and regulations established in this Law and its regulation.

Art. 1.- The SIGET, in the exercise of its functions, shall collect from commercial network operators of telecommunications the technical information that is necessary to verify compliance with the law, its regulations and the standards that are applicable.

Art. 1.- The SIGET shall carry out by itself, or through authorized and designated experts or auditors for that purpose, and supervised by SIGET personnel, the inspections it deems necessary in order to verify the truthfulness of the information provided, to the extent necessary for the exercise of its functions.

Art. 1.- The SIGET is the entity in charge of administering, managing and monitoring the radioelectric spectrum; and is the competent authority to verify the regularity of the conditions of the enabling titles, as well as to apply the sanctions or corrective measures that correspond.

Art. 2-A.- a) Equality: operators and providers must provide users of telecommunications services under the same conditions of quality, content and place where they are used.

Art. 2-A.- d) Privacy of Information: obligation of operators and providers to guarantee the right to privacy, freedom and the secrecy of communications, as well as to protect the confidentiality of the information they obtain from their users, providers or competitors, on the occasion of the provision of the services, in accordance with Art. 24 of the Constitution of the Republic of El Salvador.

Art. 5-A.- The SIGET shall establish the technical regulations based on the quality standards of public telecommunications services. Every commercial telecommunications network operator shall be obliged to: a) Have an auditable measurement system that allows verification of the quality of public telecommunications services and the verification of the measurements, in accordance with the technical regulations based on the quality standards that SIGET issues.

Art. 5-A.- The SIGET shall establish the technical regulations based on the quality standards of public telecommunications services. Every commercial telecommunications network operator shall be obliged to: b) Report in the periods indicated by the SIGET, regarding the requirements established in the technical regulations based on the Service Quality Standards, indicating non-compliances of the quality indicators related to the establishment and duration of telephone calls, data services, text and multimedia messaging, voice quality, application of tariffs, customer service, radioelectric coverage, damages and failures in networks and other services provided in telecommunications networks.

Art. 7.- Operators interested in providing telephony services shall request a concession from SIGET for the exploitation of the service, which shall be granted to them automatically for a period of thirty years upon sole fulfillment of the registration requirements to be established in the Regulation of this Law.

Art. 8.- The SIGET shall determine the maximum value both of the basic tariffs of the public fixed and mobile telephony service, as well as of the basic interconnection charges, in both cases already regulated by SIGET, according to regulatory norms based on cost studies and international price comparisons, whose methodology is recognized by the International Telecommunication Union (ITU). That methodology shall be implemented within no more than nine months counted from the entry into force of this Decree.

Art. 8.- Telecommunications operating companies shall be obliged to promptly submit to SIGET the legally audited information that it requires for the determination of the maximum tariffs and charges provided in the preceding paragraph. Failure by the operators to fulfill this obligation shall be considered a very serious infraction for the purposes of applying this law.

Art. 8.- The SIGET shall carry out an annual review of the maximum telephone tariffs and maximum interconnection charges. The first review that the SIGET is obliged to carry out, according to the provisions of this provision, shall be carried out after one year has elapsed since the implementation of the methodology for establishing tariffs and charges based on costs.

Art. 8.- The SIGET shall cause the periodic publication of the maximum tariffs of public telecommunications services after carrying out the review referred to above. Likewise, operators shall publish at least quarterly, in a written medium of wide national dissemination, the tariffs for the public telephony services they provide.

Art. 8.- The SIGET shall suspend the approval of any adjustment made to the tariffs while the affected operator is in non-compliance with any final enforceable resolution of the SIGET.

Art. 8-A.- With the objective of preventing an important provider from employing anti-competitive practices in its territory, the SIGET shall collaborate with the Superintendence of Competition so that the corresponding measures are taken.

Art. 9.- The State may grant the right to exploit portions of the radioelectric spectrum to any natural or legal person for a period of twenty years, which may be renewed, in no case automatically; the conditions and procedures established by SIGET must be complied with in the forms provided in this Law.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 1) That the contract executed between the concessionaire and the purchaser or lessee be authorized by SIGET with respect to technical aspects.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 2) That the purchaser maintain solvency of the obligations arising from the concession.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 3) That the continuity in the provision of public services is not affected with respect to the conditions originally offered to consumers.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 4) That the concessionaire and the interested party demonstrate the financial feasibility for the efficient operation of the concession.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 5) Efficient use of the spectrum regarding the utilization of the entire assigned bandwidth and the entire area of coverage granted, which shall be determined by the respective technical analysis prepared by SIGET.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 6) That the interested party and the applicant are solvent with respect to firm and enforceable State tax obligations.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 7) Compliance with the provisions established in this law that regulate the procedures, requirements and adequate conditions for the exploitation of the concession.

Art. 9-A.- Concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right to exploit concessioned frequencies, subject to compliance with the following requirements: 8) When the Superintendence considers it necessary, it shall request the opinion of the Superintendence of Competition, for which it shall have a maximum term of forty-five days.

Art. 9-A.- In any total or partial transfer of the spectrum the purchaser shall pay SIGET the administrative processing and registration costs in accordance with the cost tariff structure in force in the Registry published by SIGET, which shall be indexed each year with the CPI.

Art. 9-B.- The SIGET is the state entity responsible for the management of the radioelectric spectrum. This management covers the planning, administration, monitoring and technical control of the use and exploitation of the radioelectric spectrum, in a rational, efficient, sustainable and equitable manner, making use of the procedures and resources required by SIGET for that purpose, which may be economic, financial, legal, scientific and technical.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: a) Manage the exploitation of the radioelectric spectrum efficiently within the framework of the law, by granting the administrative acts or enabling titles of operation that may be necessary.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: b) Update the technical regulations to perform the function of supervision and surveillance of the radioelectric spectrum, to ensure compliance with the technical, legal and financial aspects of the concession.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: c) Update the economic value of the radioelectric spectrum for new concessions, according to comparative market studies.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: d) Administer the National Table of Frequency Allocation (CNAF), including its timely updating according to national needs and the competent international treaties of which the Republic of El Salvador is a signatory.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: e) Administer the National Frequency Registry (RNF) and keep it updated.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: f) Administer the national spectrum monitoring system.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: g) Coordinate with other countries the use and harmonization of the radioelectric spectrum to prevent harmful interference in a preventive or corrective manner.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: h) Order and reorganize the spectrum to guarantee its rational, efficient, economic and equitable use, in response to national needs and in accordance with technological advances.

Art. 9-C.- The SIGET, in its capacity as spectrum manager, shall carry out, among others, and following due process, the following activities for spectrum management: i) Re-attribute, reclassify, reserve, reassign and release frequencies of the radioelectric spectrum for reasons of feasibility, opportunity and convenience, objectively, promptly, transparently and non-discriminatorily, in accordance with this Law, respecting third-party rights, complying with due process and based on technical criteria.

Art. 9-C.- If in the exercise of the faculty established in literal i) of this Article the exploitation rights of concessionaires of broadcasting are affected, the SIGET shall reassign new exclusive nature frequencies in favor of the affected concessionaires, respecting their rights, attributed service, the bandwidth originally granted and the exploitation rights derived from it, maintaining the same conditions in which they were granted.

Art. 10.- It is the attribution of the SIGET to prepare and keep updated the National Table of Frequency Allocation (CNAF), a document that shall contain at least the allocation and adjudication of the different bands of the radioelectric spectrum for the different services, the conditions and classifications of their use and exploitation, according to national needs, without determining the type of technology to be used, under the principle of technological neutrality.

Art. 11.- The CNAF shall be public and of mandatory compliance and may be consulted by any person, observing the measures that, for the safeguarding and conservation of the information contained in it, the SIGET issues.

Art. 12.- Any person who wishes to exploit a part of the regulated use spectrum shall request the respective concession from SIGET.

Art. 12.- Regarding amateur radio service, radio amateurs may use the free use spectrum, subject to prior qualification by SIGET and registration in the Registry.

Art. 14.- All operators of satellite communication systems that transmit from national territory shall comply with the following requirements: a) Conform their transmissions to the standards specified by the ITU for satellite use frequencies.

Art. 14.- All operators of satellite communication systems that transmit from national territory shall comply with the following requirements: b) Have the rights to use orbital positions where required.

Art. 14.- All operators of satellite communication systems that transmit from national territory shall comply with the following requirements: c) Register their transmitting equipment in accordance with what is required in this Law.

Art. 14.- All operators of satellite communication systems that transmit from national territory shall comply with the following requirements: d) Have the spectrum use rights clearly established by treaties, agreements or international accords signed and ratified by El Salvador, or by obtaining a concession for spectrum use granted by SIGET.

Art. 14.- The installation and commissioning of earth stations for satellite communications shall require a concession for the frequencies that their emissions will use, except when the right to use such frequencies is clearly established in international conventions, agreements or treaties in force in El Salvador.

Art. 14.- When, due to force majeure or a fortuitous event, a satellite transmitting to national territory ceases operation, SIGET may grant the affected operators the change to a new frequency without the need to obtain a new concession for spectrum use.

Art. 15.- Requests for fragmentations and defragmentations of the radioelectric spectrum shall be previously submitted by the holder of the exploitation right for approval by SIGET, which shall evaluate the request in accordance with the common procedure established in this Law.

Art. 15.- Transfers of the radioelectric spectrum shall be previously submitted by the holder of the exploitation right for approval by SIGET, which shall evaluate the request in accordance with the common procedure established in this Law.

Art. 15-A.- The SIGET may request the Superintendence of Competition that, after a feasibility study in accordance with its legal framework and powers, it issue an opinion regarding the participation of certain natural or legal persons in processes for the grant of concessions for exploitation of the radioelectric spectrum, or the transfer of the exploitation right of such concessions in the secondary market, considering whether they would significantly limit the level of competition desired in the market or create barriers to entry for new competitors.

Art. 19-A.- Companies authorized by SIGET to operate a submarine cable system as a public telecommunications service shall be obliged to provide access to such systems, including platform installations, under reasonable and non-discriminatory conditions that guarantee access.

Art. 20.- Every commercial telecommunications network operator shall provide access to essential resources to any operator that requests it upon payment and without discrimination, offering disaggregated interconnection charges as well as access to disaggregated network elements. Access shall be granted with the quality and at the switching nodes requested provided that it is technically feasible.

Art. 21.- Any operator that requires access to essential resources of another commercial network operator shall present to it a contracting offer specifying prices and technical conditions. This offer shall be sent with acknowledgment of receipt. A copy of each request shall be sent to SIGET with proof of receipt by the operator to whom it was sent.

Art. 21.- Forty days after the day following the presentation of the access request, if no agreement exists in whole or in part, the interested parties may go to SIGET for conflict resolution. This period may be extended or reduced by mutual agreement.

Art. 23.- Operators of access services that have more than ten thousand lines in service shall provide to their users, without discrimination, the possibility of selecting the intermediate services provider by using an operator selection code, according to what is stipulated in the Numbering Plan. Access to intermediate services through the multi-carrier system may not be charged.

Art. 24.- Operators of access services may offer their users the pre-subscription service to access the multi-carrier system, respecting the provisions of Article 107 of this Law.

Art. 25.- When pre-subscription to intermediate services is offered by access service operators, users may specify to their access service operator the intermediate services operator with which they have chosen to subscribe.

Art. 27.- The Numbering Plan shall be developed and administered by SIGET.

Art. 27.- Operators that require the assignment of telephone numbers for their users shall request them from SIGET.

Art. 27.- SIGET shall grant numbers based on batches. Within five days following the submission of the request, SIGET may grant what was requested or, if it considers that there is a risk of exhausting the Numbering Plan, shall call for an auction of its grant according to the procedure stipulated in Article 102 of this Law.

Art. 27.- All telecommunications operators shall obey the rules established in the Numbering Plan.

Art. 28.- The operator selection codes for the multi-carrier service shall be granted by SIGET by means of an auction, which shall be carried out in accordance with the procedure established in Article 102 of this Law.

Art. 29.- j) Portability of the telephone number; this is established as a right of users and an obligation of fixed and mobile telephony operators; in particular, number portability consists of a user being able to voluntarily change operator while retaining their mobile or fixed number at no cost. Likewise, it is an obligation of the origin operator to carry out, promptly and at no cost to the user, all procedures for the migration of the user to the operator he has selected.

Art. 29.- The SIGET shall be responsible for conducting the feasibility study for the selection of types of portability and the model of system to be implemented and shall issue the corresponding regulation within no more than six months counted from the entry into force of this decree.

Art. 29.- m) The operator is obliged to inform the user, by appropriate procedure and technique, of the available balance contained in the acquired card, pins, electronic recharges and balance transfers, according to their value, ensuring that its debit corresponds to the exact time used.

Art. 30.- a) Users must pay installation fees and punctually pay invoices arising from the provision of the service.

Art. 30.- c) Users must obtain authorization from the access service operator prior to utilization or connection with its network, for which they must subscribe a service contract with said operator.

Art. 30.- d) Users must present the documentation that allows identification of the applicant for telecommunications services in the prepaid payment modality.

Art. 30.- e) Users must inform in writing the operator that provides services in the prepaid modality of any modification in the ownership of the service.

Art. 30-A.- It is an obligation of access service operators to maintain a registry of all users, including prepaid users, and to keep such information available to the competent authority in criminal investigations that requires it.

Art. 30-A.- The delivery of information required by the Attorney General's Office, related to registration data of the investigated line or lines, call records for a clearly determined period, as well as data on the origin of any other type of electronic communication, and the temporary suspension of lines, blocking of telephone devices and other electronic communications, shall be carried out in accordance with the provisions of Art. 238 of the Criminal Procedure Code.

Art. 31.- Operators may suspend the provision of their services without prior notice to the user only in the following cases: a) When installation rights or billings or installments of two or more months derived from the provision of the service are pending payment.

Art. 31.- Operators may suspend the provision of their services without prior notice to the user only in the following cases: d) When the ownership of the service in the prepaid modality is transferred without the respective written notification to the corresponding operator.

Art. 115.- The exploitation of the radio-electric spectrum for the provision of sound broadcasting services and television broadcasting services, both free reception and by subscription, requires a concession. The application for this concession must be made in accordance with the general rules applicable to regulated use frequencies, specifying the service to be provided and its coverage area. These concessions will be granted for a term of twenty years, automatically renewable for equal periods. Sound broadcasting services and television by wired means require a license, which must be granted by SIGET. For the use of these concessions and licenses, the concessionaires and licensees must prove to SIGET that they have the permissions or authorizations of the program owners in order to proceed with their transmission.

Art. 115-A.- The concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right of exploitation of concessioned frequencies provided that the concessionaires comply with the following requirement 2: That the acquirer maintain solvency of the obligations derived from the concession.

Art. 115-A.- The concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right of exploitation of concessioned frequencies provided that the concessionaires comply with the following requirement 3: That the continuity in the provision of the service not be affected with respect to the conditions originally offered to consumers.

Art. 115-A.- The concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right of exploitation of concessioned frequencies provided that the concessionaires comply with the following requirement 4: Efficient use of the spectrum relative to the utilization of the entire assigned bandwidth and the entire area of coverage granted, which will be determined by the respective technical analysis prepared by SIGET.

Art. 115-A.- The concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right of exploitation of concessioned frequencies provided that the concessionaires comply with the following requirement 6: Compliance with the provisions set forth in this law that regulate the procedures, requirements and adequate conditions for the exploitation of the concession.

Art. 115-A.- The concessionaires may transfer to any title or lease the right of exploitation of concessioned frequencies provided that the concessionaires comply with the following requirement 7: Where applicable, when the Superintendency deems it necessary, request an opinion from the Superintendency of Competition, for which it will have a maximum term of forty-five days; if the opinion determines that it does not generate concentration, the corresponding procedure will proceed.

Art. 116.- The titles enabling the use of the radio-electric spectrum for sound broadcasting services and television shall cause rates whose amount must be paid annually to SIGET to cover the costs related to the tasks of management, administration and surveillance of the radio-electric spectrum. Payments shall be made in the month of October of each year; the values to be paid are the result of the following formula: TA = CUE * Fs * AB * Pac * n. Where: TA = Annual Rate; CUE = Unit Cost of the Spectrum, in colones ¢11.9358, in dollars = USD$1.36408/MHz/inhabitant/month, whose value must be indexed annually with the official CPI factor; Fs = Service Factor, for AM equals 0.003, FM equals 0.00029, TV equals 0.000122; AB = Bandwidth in MHz; Pac = Population to be covered; n = Months of the year, 12. In no case, when applying the above formula, may the values for the annual rate be less than $250 in Amplitude Modulation (AM), $500 in Frequency Modulation (FM) and $1,500 in Television.

Art. 118.- The frequencies assigned to free reception broadcasting services or sound and television broadcasting must be used efficiently and rationally; for this purpose SIGET shall prepare the utilization plan of the bands of the Radio-Electric Spectrum attributed for such service by the ITU. In order to avoid harmful interference problems and to promote a better use of the Radio-Electric Spectrum attributed to the free reception broadcasting service in sound broadcasting, the minimum separation between adjacent channels shall be 30 KHz in Amplitude Modulation, AM, 525 – 1705 KHz; and 400 KHz in Frequency Modulation, 88-108 MHz.

Art. 118.- The frequencies assigned to free reception broadcasting services or sound and television broadcasting: The frequency 88.1 MHz is declared of Official Use and its use is authorized to the Legislative Assembly of El Salvador, which prior to its use shall communicate to SIGET the technical operating parameters, which must be electromagnetically compatible and free of interference with other legally established stations.

Art. 118.- The frequencies assigned to free reception broadcasting services or sound and television broadcasting: The frequency 88.1 MHz may not commence operations without SIGET having previously carried out an inspection in order to verify that the installations comply with the technical parameters that guarantee electromagnetic compatibility with other legally established stations and permit the start of operations, in accordance with the provisions of Art. 123 of the Regulation of this Law.

Art. 119.- For the assignment of channels or broadcasting frequencies, SIGET shall issue the necessary rules and regulations, avoiding causing harmful interference to broadcasting stations that are legally operating. SIGET shall monitor, inspect and audit the operation of broadcasting stations on a permanent basis, and shall also require concessionaires the necessary information in order to verify the proper exploitation of the frequency, the provision of the attributed service and compliance with the other parameters and conditions established in the contract and this Law.

Art. 121.- Every subscription broadcasting operator, sound or television, is obliged to facilitate the inspection tasks of SIGET, by allowing access to its installations as requested, as well as to supply all technical information directly related to the operation.

Art. 123.- Concessions and licenses for Free Reception Broadcasting services shall only be granted to natural persons by birth or Salvadoran legal persons. In the case of Salvadoran legal persons, the share capital must be constituted at least fifty-one percent by Salvadorans. This share capital and its amendments must be reported to SIGET.

Art. 124.- The following are specific causes for the revocation of enabling titles: b) The ""non-use of assigned frequencies"" for a period of six months, except in cases of force majeure or fortuitous event.

Art. 124.- The following are specific causes for the revocation of enabling titles: c) Non-compliance with the provisions stipulated in the concession contract regarding the concessioned technical conditions and parameters and the provisions of the applicable legislation.

Art. 124.- The following are specific causes for the revocation of enabling titles: d) Not performing the operation and provision of the subscription television broadcasting service by wired and wireless means, in the entirety of the schedules, the authorized coverage areas and what is established in this Law.

Art. 124.- The following are specific causes for the revocation of enabling titles: e) Not having the respective permits and authorizations from SIGET for licenses of the distribution service of subscription television.

Art. 125.- The concessionaire may renounce his right of exploitation of the spectrum at any stage during the term of the concession. In such case, the rules provided for early renewal shall apply.

Art. 125-A.- When the concessionaire submits a request for link frequencies to transmit the signals to the main transmitting stations or repeaters in the authorized zones, SIGET shall carry out the technical feasibility study. If the result of the study is favorable, SIGET shall grant the corresponding concessions within the term established in the Law.

Art. 126.- SIGET shall grant concessions for the link frequencies used to transmit. The authorization for these link and repeater frequencies for radio and television shall not cause any additional payment. The rights of third parties derived from acts entered into with concessionaires that have requested the extension shall be subject to the second paragraph of Art. 15 of this Law.

Art. 128.- The inspections and supervisions provided by SIGET to the concessionaires and licensees referred to in this regime shall not cause any fee.

Art. 129.- State broadcasting stations, and community stations including religious, ethnic, among others, that are non-profit, shall be exempt from paying the annual fee in concept of management of the spectrum of broadcasting stations and their links referred to in Art. 116 of this Law.

Art. 98.- The operator shall resolve, within a maximum period of ten working days and at no cost, the complaints filed by users of fixed and mobile telephony services.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: a) BILLING: Amounts appearing on the bill or payment receipt that are being contested and with respect to which the user does not recognize the consumption of the service or the title from which the operator has the right to bill them; in the case of complaints for billing of supplementary or additional services, the billing complaint shall include the amounts for the concept claimed that have been billed, even in bills prior to the bill with respect to which the complaint is filed.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: b) CHARGING FOR THE SERVICE WITHOUT COST: Amounts charged to the user for the public service of fixed and mobile telephony that correspond to concepts different from those timely billed, and with respect to which the user does not recognize the consumption of the service or the title from which the operator has the right to charge them.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: c) INSTALLATION OR ACTIVATION OF THE SERVICE: Failure of the operator to carry out the installation or activation of the service to which it committed at the time of contracting or when the operator established a term longer than that permitted by the regulations in force to proceed with such installation or activation; includes also the operator's failure to activate or deactivate supplementary or additional services that had been requested by the user.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: d) TRANSFER OF THE SERVICE: Failure of the operator to carry out the transfer of the service on the date on which it had committed or when in response to a transfer request there is no response or the user is not satisfied with it.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: e) SUSPENSION OR CUT OF THE SERVICE: Cases in which the user considers that he has suffered an unjustified suspension or that his service has been cut without justified cause.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: f) QUALITY OR FITNESS IN THE PROVISION OF THE SERVICE INCLUDING TRUTHFULNESS OF THE INFORMATION PROVIDED TO THE USER: problems derived from inadequate network operation that generate user dissatisfaction, such as imperceptible communication, noise and interference on the line, the inability to be heard and intermittent service. Likewise, problems of quality of public fixed and mobile telephone services shall be considered those arising as a consequence of the provision of the service itself or the failure to inform users truthfully about the service or about the status of the reports they have made.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: g) Failure to deliver the bill or a copy of it requested by the user.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: h) Failure by the Operator to make refunds ordered by SIGET in favor of users.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: i) PHYSICAL OR VIRTUAL PAYMENT CARDS: Problems arising from the provision of services through payment card systems, such as service enablement, the credit that the payment card grants, among others.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: j) Refusal of the Operator to contract with the service applicant.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: k) Other measures indicated by SIGET.

Art. 98.- The operator shall accept complaints from users in the following cases: l) For failure in the provision of telecommunications services instituted in the technical regulations based on the quality of service standards established by SIGET.

Art. 98.- The operators shall maintain a Complaint Registration System in which the general data of complaints made by users shall be recorded.

Art. 98.- The complaint filed, as well as notifications, evidentiary means and other documents related to it, shall form a file whose organization and conservation shall be the responsibility of the operators. The operators shall assign a code or complaint number to the file, which shall be maintained during the administrative procedure.

Art. 98.- The operators shall adopt the necessary measures in order to preserve the unity and integrity of the complaint file.

Art. 98.- The parties have the right to know at any time the status of the processing of their complaints, as well as to have access to the corresponding file. The parties may request that a simple or certified copy of the pieces of the corresponding file be issued for the complaint procedure.

Art. 98.- The resolution of the complaint, favorable or unfavorable, must be reasoned and signed by the responsible official(s), with express indication of each of the evidentiary means acted upon that support the decision and of the legal norms applied in the resolution of each case, and the notification of the resolution of the complaint shall be communicated in writing to the affected user, the day after the deadline expires, sending a copy of it to SIGET; such notification shall be made at the address where the bills for the claimed service are sent or at the address that the user has indicated in writing during the complaint procedure.

Art. 98.- If the user does not accept the solution to his complaint proposed by the operator, he may file a complaint with the Consumer Defender (Defensoría del Consumidor). SIGET, at the request of the Consumer Defender, must present a technical report on the complaint within a maximum period of eight working days. For this purpose the operator shall be obliged to provide SIGET, at its request and within a period not greater than three working days after receipt of the request, the documentary support of the charges or conditions of service provision, as the case may be. Likewise, the operator shall send the file corresponding to the complaint.

Art. 98.- If the resolution issued by the authorities that oversee consumer protection is favorable to the user, the operator shall be obliged to compensate him for the damages it has caused him. In cases of undue charges, it shall refund the amount that the user has paid to the operator, without prejudice to the corresponding legal actions.

Art. 85-K.- The effects of the award shall be conditioned upon payment for the concession, which must be made within a period of five days. Payment shall be by certified or cashier's check, electronic payment or bank transfer. When the awardee does not make the payment within the stipulated period, he shall lose the guarantee presented as a condition of participation in the auction, these funds entering SIGET's patrimony. In turn SIGET shall revoke the award without further formality. SIGET shall retain fifty percent of the final amount resulting from the auction, which shall be entered into its patrimony, transferring the rest to the Special Fund of Resources Originating from the Privatization of the National Administration of Telecommunications.

If, once the deadline has passed, the concessionaire does not submit the request for an extension, it shall be understood that the concessionaire has no interest in continuing to exploit the spectrum; consequently, the concession shall be extinguished on the date of its expiration, in which case the General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications (SIGET) may dispose of the spectrum that was assigned to such concessions, applying the applicable legal framework and following due process.

The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications (SIGET), through the Telecommunications Management, shall render an ex ante technical report on compliance with the aforementioned conditions.

If the technical report is favorable, the Superintendent shall issue the corresponding resolution within ten days, under the technical terms and conditions duly updated in the Registry.

Upon receipt of notification of the resolution, within ten days, the Superintendent and the concessionaire shall sign the respective concession extension contract, and the Superintendent and the concessionaire shall ensure that the said contract specifies the technical terms and conditions duly updated in the Registry to be complied with in accordance with the cited judgment, this Law and its regulation.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications (SIGET) is authorized to prepare, within no more than one year from the entry into force of this decree, the National Plan of Terrestrial Digital Television.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications shall, in preparing the National Plan of Terrestrial Digital Television, include the process for the selection of the standard to be adopted in the country.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications shall, in preparing the National Plan of Terrestrial Digital Television, include the respective Transition Plan from Analog Television to Digital Television.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications shall, in preparing the National Plan of Terrestrial Digital Television, include, associated therewith, the implementation of the Digital Dividend.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications shall, in preparing the National Plan of Terrestrial Digital Television, include the corresponding regulations.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications (SIGET) shall relocate the new frequencies to the television operators affected by Terrestrial Digital Television.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications shall ensure, in the aforementioned relocation, respect for the rights of the concessionaires affected who must migrate to other frequencies, the bandwidth originally granted and the exploitation rights derived therefrom, with legal certainty of maintaining their broadcasts under equal or similar conditions to those in which they had been developing them.

Art. 35.- The General Superintendency of Electricity and Telecommunications shall ensure that concessionaires affected who must migrate to other frequencies enjoy preference in the reassignment of the same bandwidth and registration in the National Frequency Attribution Chart, with respect to those concessionaires whose frequencies will not suffer any alteration with the implementation of Terrestrial Digital Television."
