(El Salvador 2024)_01,GOV_REGULATOR,True,1.0,True,1.0,"Art. 2.- Scope of application. The bodies of Government, their dependencies, official autonomous institutions, municipal authorities or any other entity or organism, regardless of their form, nature or legal situation, through which public resources are administered, State assets are held, acts of public administration in general are executed or that have an impact on the nation's critical infrastructures, are obligated to comply with this law.
Funds originating from Agreements or Treaties entered into by the State of El Salvador with other States or with International Organizations are included within public resources, unless the Agreement or Treaty establishes higher requirements in the matter of cybersecurity or information security.
Consequently, all public servants, inside or outside the territory of the Republic, and the persons who work in the entities mentioned in this article, are obligated to the full compliance with this law.

Art. 3.- Guiding principles a) The subjects established within the scope of application of this law shall apply a security by design approach in the development, purchase, provisioning, implementation and management of computer systems or technological equipment and in the processes of administration thereof, so that cybersecurity and information security are the original, transversal and permanent objective during their operation or use.

Art. 3.- Guiding principles b) The measures or actions adopted pursuant to this law shall have as their purpose the reduction of the adverse effects of cybersecurity or information security incidents, recovery within the shortest possible time and the continuity and promotion of the resilience of the critical activities that depend on the compromised information systems.

Art. 3.- Guiding principles e) The measures or actions in the face of cybersecurity or information security threats or incidents shall be adopted and carried out in a timely and diligent manner, in order to avoid the increase of damages and vulnerabilities to systems, information and services, as well as the possible affectation of other areas or operations.

Art. 3.- Guiding principles f) The subjects obliged by this law shall carry out all possible and necessary measures to safeguard the security of the networks, equipment and computer systems that are used for the exercise of their activities and functions, as well as those in which they store, process and manage their information or that of their users.

Art. 3.- Guiding principles g) The measures or actions implemented in response to cybersecurity or information security threats or incidents shall be applied in an equitable, fair and adequate manner according to the level of exposure to the risks inherent to them, the damages they cause or their eventual social and economic impact.

Art. 3.- Guiding principles h) Confidentiality: The subjects obliged by this law shall implement the measures, actions or tools necessary to ensure that computer systems, networks or information are only accessible to employees, officials, authorities or users authorized to do so, thereby protecting the privacy of citizens.

Art. 6.- Obligations a) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall implement a permanent cybersecurity and information security management system in order to determine and mitigate those risks that may affect the security of computer systems and infrastructures, covering equipment, networks, telecommunications infrastructures or processes executed or implemented therein.

Art. 6.- Obligations b) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall prepare an information technology security and information security strategy in accordance with national and international standards or reference frameworks.

Art. 6.- Obligations c) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall maintain an updated record of all actions carried out that compose the cybersecurity and information security management system.

Art. 6.- Obligations d) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall develop and implement operational continuity and cybersecurity plans, which must be approved by the competent authority and will be subject to periodic reviews in accordance with applicable guidelines.

Art. 6.- Obligations e) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall continuously carry out review operations, exercises, drills and analyses of the measures, computer systems, equipment, networks, infrastructures or processes executed or implemented therein; all in order to detect elements or risks that compromise cybersecurity and information security.

Art. 6.- Obligations f) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall apply and comply immediately and effectively with the measures necessary to prevent, report and resolve cybersecurity and information security threats, in accordance with the legal provisions established in this regard.

Art. 6.- Obligations g) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall adopt in a timely, expeditious and efficient manner the necessary actions to reduce the impact and propagation of a cybersecurity or information security incident.

Art. 6.- Obligations h) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall submit reports related to cybersecurity and information security that are required by the competent authority in the time, form and specificity required.

Art. 6.- Obligations i) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall create or designate an area or areas responsible for the implementation, monitoring, review and adaptation of actions and measures related to cybersecurity and information security, granting them internally the necessary independence and powers to perform their functions without obstacles or prior procedures, but in close coordination with the competent authority in cybersecurity and information security matters.

Art. 6.- Obligations j) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall comply with the information requirements or instructions issued by the competent authority, in the corresponding time, form and specificities.

Art. 6.- Obligations k) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall facilitate the management, audit and inspection tasks carried out by the competent authority in cybersecurity and information security matters.

Art. 6.- Obligations l) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall inform potential affected parties, insofar as they can be identified and when so determined by the State Cybersecurity Agency, about the occurrence of incidents that could compromise or seriously compromise their information, as well as possible measures that they may adopt to mitigate the risks caused by them, if any. This obligation to inform shall be fulfilled through the means or mechanisms determined by the said authority.

Art. 6.- Obligations m) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall carry out the planning, management and procedures necessary to implement the obligations arising from this law, as well as from the provisions issued pursuant to it, in a timely manner.

Art. 6.- Obligations n) The subjects to whom this law applies in accordance with Article 2 shall comply with the other obligations established in the provisions contained in policies, regulations, protocols, guidelines, standards and technical criteria in cybersecurity and information security.

Art. 6.- Obligations. The competent authority shall issue the pertinent provisions in cybersecurity and information security in accordance with that established in letter b) of article 8 of this law, so that the subjects obliged by this law remain in strict coordination therewith and comply with the obligations established in this article.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall a) Elaborate the Nation's Cybersecurity and Information Security Policy, which shall contain the guidelines, plans and action programs that will be applied for its compliance, and submit it for approval to the President of the Republic.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall b) Issue regulations, protocols, guidelines, standards and technical criteria, both general and specific, based on best practices and international reference frameworks in cybersecurity and information security; in accordance with Article 5 and in consonance with the Nation's Cybersecurity and Information Security Policy.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall c) Create and implement the action programs necessary to respond to cybersecurity and information security threats or incidents that involve the subjects obliged by this law.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall d) Require the entities obliged by this law whose computer systems, equipment, networks or infrastructures have been affected by a cybersecurity or information security incident to carry out the actions necessary for the fulfillment of its purposes.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall e) Create and administer a National Register of Cybersecurity Threats and Incidents.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall f) Qualify, by reasoned resolution, the operators of critical infrastructures, and submit said qualification for ratification by the President of the Republic.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall g) Withdraw the qualification, by reasoned resolution, from the operators of critical infrastructures, and submit said withdrawal for ratification by the President of the Republic.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall h) Require the entities obliged by this law that have suffered a cybersecurity or information security incident to deliver to potential affected parties or investigative authorities truthful, sufficient and timely information about such circumstance, in accordance with the provisions of letter l) of article 6.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall i) Design and implement citizen training, capacity-building, strengthening, dissemination and promotion campaigns on cybersecurity and information security culture.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall j) Require, from the subjects obliged by this law, access to the information necessary to prevent the occurrence of cybersecurity or information security incidents and to manage threats or incidents that have already occurred. When the said information includes personal data, such data shall be processed with the purpose of anonymizing their holders, whenever possible, without hampering incident management.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall k) Establish coordination and collaboration mechanisms between the area or areas responsible for the implementation, monitoring, review and adaptation of the actions and measures related to cybersecurity and information security that the subjects obliged by this law shall establish.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall l) Establish the technical requirements and the manner of carrying out audits and risk assessments on cybersecurity and information security that it must perform to guarantee compliance with this law and other provisions issued in said field.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall m) Carry out reviews of the measures and actions of cybersecurity and information security implemented by the subjects obliged by this law, in order to establish improvement and risk prevention mechanisms, ensuring the use of adequate and updated measures or tools to respond to threats arising from the use of new technologies.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall n) Foster cooperation with international organizations and foreign authorities on matters related to cybersecurity and information security.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall o) Represent the country before International Organizations related to cybersecurity and information security policies.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall p) Impose sanctions in accordance with this law.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall q) Issue, modify or annul provisional measures as provided in the Administrative Procedure Law.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall r) Study and submit to consideration of the Executive Branch, through the Ministry of Justice and Public Security, proposals for laws or reforms to existing provisions on cybersecurity and information security.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall s) Approve the internal regulations, as well as manuals and other instruments necessary for the administration of the Agency.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall u) Assist the Attorney General's Office, the Judiciary and other public institutions that request it, in forensic expertise activities related to the functions it performs.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall v) Notify the Attorney General's Office within a maximum period of seventy-two hours when, in the exercise of its powers or on account of them, it becomes aware that there are elements that could configure any crime contemplated in the Special Law against Computer and Related Crimes or other criminal laws.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall w) Define and coordinate the implementation of training and awareness programs related to cybersecurity and information security; for which it may be supported by other public institutions or private entities or national or international organizations.

Art. 8.- The Agency shall x) Exercise the other powers or faculties that legally correspond or are necessary for compliance with this law or others that apply to it.

Art. 16.- Confidentiality. The officials and employees of the Agency shall have the obligation to keep secret and strict confidentiality of the information to which they have access by virtue of the functions and work activities they perform; this obligation shall remain even after having ceased in the position for a minimum period of five years.

Art. 19.- Minor infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following minor infractions: a) To submit, outside the deadline, the reports or information related to cybersecurity and information security that are required by the Agency, provided that such information is not related to an incident or necessary for its management.

Art. 19.- Minor infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following minor infractions: c) To fail to comply with the provisions contained in policies, technical regulations, guidelines and instructions in cybersecurity and information security, when these do not affect the implementation of the cybersecurity and information security management system or incident management.

Art. 20.- Serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following serious infractions: a) Not having an updated record of all actions executed that compose the cybersecurity and information security management system.

Art. 20.- Serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following serious infractions: b) Not preparing and sending, in time and form, the operational continuity and cybersecurity plans for the Agency's approval.

Art. 20.- Serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following serious infractions: c) Not implementing the operational continuity and cybersecurity plans approved by the Agency.

Art. 20.- Serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following serious infractions: d) Not continuously carrying out review operations, exercises, drills and analyses of the measures, computer systems, equipment, networks, infrastructures or processes executed or implemented therein.

Art. 20.- Serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following serious infractions: e) Failing to comply with the provisions contained in policies, technical regulations, guidelines and instructions in cybersecurity and information security, when these relate to the implementation of the cybersecurity and information security management system or incident management.

Art. 21.- Very serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following very serious infractions: c) Not implementing a permanent cybersecurity and information security management system, without justified cause, in accordance with that established by the Agency.

Art. 21.- Very serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following very serious infractions: d) Failing to comply with the measures necessary to prevent, report and resolve cybersecurity and information security threats, in an immediate and effective manner.

Art. 21.- Very serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following very serious infractions: e) Not adopting, in a timely, expeditious and efficient manner, the necessary actions to reduce the impact and propagation of a cybersecurity or information security incident.

Art. 21.- Very serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following very serious infractions: f) Not creating or designating an area or areas responsible for the implementation, monitoring, review and adaptation of actions and measures related to cybersecurity and information security.

Art. 21.- Very serious infractions. The subjects to whom this law applies shall be considered to commit the following very serious infractions: h) Not informing potential affected parties, insofar as they can be identified and when so determined by the Agency, about the occurrence of incidents that could compromise or seriously compromise their information, as well as the possible measures that they may adopt to mitigate the risks caused by them, if any.

Art. 25.- Inability to belong to the public sector. An official or employee who is dismissed or removed from his position for the commission of any of the infractions provided for in this law may not work for the public administration for a period of ten years following the imposition of the respective sanction.

Art. 28.- Sanctioning procedure. The procedure for the determination of the infractions and sanctions established by this law shall be carried out in accordance with the simplified procedure contemplated in the Administrative Procedure Law.
The procedure may be initiated ex officio, by notice, by complaint or by any other means.
When the information about the alleged commission of the infraction is received verbally or in such a way that there is no written backup thereof, the Agency shall draw up a record with all pertinent data for the processing of the procedure.

Art. 31.- Issuance of applicable regulations and provisions. The Agency shall prepare the regulations, protocols, guidelines, standards and technical criteria, both general and specific, in cybersecurity and information security, in accordance with Article 5, no later than ninety days counted from the entry into force of this law."
