Article 1 69. ( FALSE T ESTIMONY). A ny witness, e xpert, i nterpreter, t ranslator o r a ny o ther p erson w ho i s questioned i n a j udicial o r a dministrative p rocess, w ho s tates a f alsehood or d enies o r c onceals t he t ruth, i n whole o r i n p art, of w hat he knows about t he fact o r w hat c oncerns i t, shall b e s ubject t o i mprisonment o f o ne (1) t o fifteen (15) m onths.

Article 1 68. (SELF-CALUMNY). Anyone who, t hrough a s tatement o r c onfession m ade b efore t he competent authority t o c onduct t he first judicial police proceedings o r t o instruct the p rocess, f alsely a ccuses himself of h aving c ommitted a c rime o f p ublic a ction o r a c rime o f t he same nature perpetrated b y another, s hall be p unished w ith work performance o f o ne (1) month to o ne (1) year.

Article 72. ( Objectivity). Prosecutors s hall ensure e ffective c ompliance w ith t he g uarantees r ecognized b y the Political Constitution o f t he State, t he i nternational C onventions and Treaties in f orce, a nd t he laws. I n their investigation, t hey s hall t ake into account n ot o nly the c ircumstances t hat allow t he accusation t o b e proven, b ut also those that s erve to exonerate t he accused from responsibility; formulating their requests in accordance with this criterion.

Article 73. ( Reasoned a ctions). Prosecutors shall f ormulate t heir requests a nd resolutions i n a r easoned a nd specific m anner. T hey s hall p roceed orally i n hearings a nd in the t rial a nd, in writing, in other c ases.

Article 115. ( Interrogations). When dealing with persons who cannot easily express themselves in Spanish or who suffer from a manifest impediment, the judge or court, ex officio or at the request of a party, shall arrange for the necessary measures so that those being questioned may be assisted by an interpreter or translator, or may express themselves in writing or in a manner that facilitates the conduct of the procedure.

Article 117. (Orality). Persons who are questioned must respond orally and without consulting notes or documents, with the exception of experts and those who are authorized to do so due to their conditions or the nature of the facts. When the procedure is carried out in writing, in the cases permitted by this Code, the questions and answers will be recorded, using the declarant's expressions.

Article 116. (Publicity). The investigating judge or the sentencing judge or court may order, by means of a reasoned resolution, that certain acts of the proceedings be carried out confidentially, in whole or in part, when: 1) The modesty or private life of any of the parties or of another person cited is affected.

Article 92. ( Preliminary w arnings). Before b eginning t he s tatement, t he a ccused s hall b e i nformed of the a ct a ttributed to h im, with a ll t he circumstances of time, p lace and manner of its commission, i ncluding those that are important for t he legal q ualification, a s ummary o f the content of t he existing evidence and the p enal provisions t hat are deemed applicable. You will b e warned that you may d ecline to testify a nd that this d ecision c annot be u sed a gainst you. The police may only question t he accused in t he presence of the prosecutor and his defense lawyer, except to verify his identity.

Article 98. ( Recording of the statement). The statements of the accused in the preparatory stage shall be recorded in written minutes or another form of record that reproduces as faithfully as possible what occurred at the hearing; the hearing shall end with the reading and signing of the minutes by all parties or with the measures arranged to guarantee the individualization, fidelity and inalterability of the other means of recording. If the accused refuses to testify, this will be recorded in the minutes. If he refuses or is unable to sign it, the reason will be stated. The statement or, where appropriate, the certificate of non-appearance shall be submitted together with the accusation.

Article 99. (Confrontation of the accused). The confrontation of the accused with witnesses or other defendants is a voluntary act. If the accused accepts it, all the rules established for his statement will apply.

Article 282. (Undercover agent). The decision of the investigating judge authorizing the intervention will record the alleged identity of the agent, which will be kept confidential and will be kept outside the proceedings with due security in a sealed envelope that will also contain the true identity of the agent.

Article 293. ( Preliminary proceedings). The accused and his/her defense attorney may participate in all proceedings carried out by the Police and will have access to all investigations carried out, except when they are confidential, as established in this Code.

Article 296. ( Apprehension). In cases where this Code authorizes the apprehension of defendants, members of the police must comply with the following basic principles of action: 4) Not to allow detainees to be presented to any media without their express consent, which will be given in the presence of the defense attorney and will be recorded in the respective proceedings;

Article 296. ( Apprehension). In cases where this Code authorizes the apprehension of defendants, members of the police must comply with the following basic principles of action: 5) Identify yourself, through your credentials at the time of arrest, as a police authority, stating your first and last name and verify the identity of the person or persons against whom the proceedings are being carried out;

Article 296. ( Apprehension). In cases where this Code authorizes the apprehension of defendants, members of the police must comply with the following basic principles of action: 6) Inform the person, at the time of arrest, of the reason for it, that he has the right to remain silent without prejudice to the right to appoint a defense attorney;

Article 296. ( Apprehension). In cases where this Code authorizes the apprehension of defendants, members of the police must comply with the following basic principles of action: 7) Inform relatives or other persons related to the accused of the arrest and the establishment where he will be taken;

Article 296. ( Apprehension). In cases where this Code authorizes the apprehension of defendants, members of the police must comply with the following basic principles of action: 8) Record in an unalterable register the place, day and time of the arrest.

Article 3 31. ( Participation of t he m edia). The j udge or court shall a uthorize the installation o f recording, p hotography, radio, f ilming o r other e quipment in the room, so that t hese means of information d o n ot harm t he development of the debate, provided t hat it is n ot a trial o f minors.

Article 3 32. ( Prohibitions o n a ccess). T he following m ay n ot enter the courtroom:
1) Minors u nder t welve (12) y ears of age, except w hen accompanied b y a n a dult w ho is responsible for t heir c onduct;
and,
2) Persons carrying b anners, u nion, p arty or a ssociation i nsignia, or members of the Armed Forces or the National Police w ho a re in uniform, u nless t hey are p erforming s urveillance f unctions.

Article 3 64. (Effects of a cquittal). T he a cquittal judgment shall order t he i mmediate r elease o f the a ccused, the cessation o f all p ersonal p recautionary measures, a nd s hall set the c osts a nd, w here appropriate, shall declare the accusation t o b e r eckless or m alicious for t he p urposes o f the c orresponding l iability.
The release of t he accused w ill be o rdered even when t he a cquittal j udgment is not f inal a nd w ill b e c arried out directly f rom the courtroom.
The judge or court, at t he request of the acquitted person, shall order the publication of the operative part of the acquittal judgment i n a written m edium o f national c irculation at the e xpense o f the s tate or t he p rivate plaintiff.

Article 3 71. (Forms o f registration). When the trial is recorded by audiovisual m eans, t he j udge o r p resident o f t he c ourt s hall o rder t he a ppropriate measures t o ensure its preservation, f idelity and authenticity, w hich must be r ecorded in minutes t hat w ill be s igned by t he j udge or m embers of t he c ourt, the s ecretary and t he p arties.
The judge o r the p resident of t he c ourt may a llow t he p arties, at t heir o wn expense, to r ecord t he p rogress o f t he t rial by a ny means.

Article 4 40. (Criminal Records Registry). The Judicial Registry of Criminal Records, dependent on the Judicial Council, shall be in charge of the centralized registry of the following resolutions: 1) Final convictions;

Article 4 40. (Criminal Records Registry). The Judicial Registry of Criminal Records, dependent on the Judicial Council, shall be in charge of the centralized registry of the following resolutions: 2) Those who declare rebellion; and,

Article 4 40. (Criminal Records Registry). The Judicial Registry of Criminal Records, dependent on the Judicial Council, shall be in charge of the centralized registry of the following resolutions: 3) Those that conditionally suspend the process.

Article 4 40. (Criminal Records Registry). Every judge or court shall submit to the registry a certified copy of these resolutions.

Article 4 40. (Criminal Records Registry). The Judicial Council shall appoint a director in charge of the registry and regulate its organization and operation.

Article 4 41. (Cancellation of criminal records). Regarding the conditional suspension of the process, your registration will be cancelled by order of the judge who issued it upon expiration of the probationary period.

Article 4 42. (Confidentiality of information). The Registry shall be confidential and may only provide reports on the resolutions indicated in Article 4 40 of this Code at the request of: 1) The interested party;

Article 4 42. (Confidentiality of information). The Registry shall be confidential and may only provide reports on the resolutions indicated in Article 4 40 of this Code at the request of: 2) The Legislative Commissions;

Article 4 42. (Confidentiality of information). The Registry shall be confidential and may only provide reports on the resolutions indicated in Article 4 40 of this Code at the request of: 3) Judges and prosecutors throughout the country; and

Article 4 42. (Confidentiality of information). The Registry shall be confidential and may only provide reports on the resolutions indicated in Article 4 40 of this Code at the request of: 4) Foreign authorities in accordance with the rules of international judicial cooperation established in this Code.

Fourth. (Transitional organic provisions). Two (2) weeks before the full validity of this Code, the Superior Courts will send the proceedings in progress to the liquidating courts and tribunals.

Fourth. (Transitional organic provisions). Once the cases have been received, the liquidating courts and tribunals, in the case of proceedings without procedural activity, will order their publication in a national circulation medium, urging the parties to continue the process within a period of three (3) months, under warning that the criminal action will be declared extinguished. After this period has expired, without the warning being served, the extinction of the criminal action will be ordered."
