(Malawi 2017)_01,GOV_REGULATOR,True,1.0,True,1.0,"6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (1) The Authority shall regulate and monitor the provision of communications services and ensure that, as far as it is practicable, reliable and affordable communications services are provided throughout Malawi and are sufficient to meet the demand for such services in accordance with the principles of transparency, certainty, market orientation, efficiency, and consumer satisfaction.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(a) The Authority shall be vested with the legal ownership of spectrum on behalf of the Republic of Malawi.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(b) The Authority shall grant licences for the provision of communications services.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(c) The Authority shall protect the interests of consumers, purchasers and other users of communications services.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(d) The Authority shall promote access to information and the development of human resources in the communications sector.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(e) The Authority shall promote efficiency and competition among entities engaged in the provision of communications services or in the supply of communication equipment.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(f) The Authority shall encourage the introduction of new technology and communications services in accordance with recognized international standard and applicable international law.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(g) The Authority shall promote research and development in communications services.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(h) The Authority shall foster the development of communication service technologies in accordance with recognized international standards and applicable international law.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(i) The Authority shall advise the Government on policy issues relating to the communications sector.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(j) The Authority shall monitor the activities of licensees to ensure compliance with the Act and the terms and conditions of their licences.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(k) The Authority shall establish a type approval regime for terminal equipment or any other electronic equipment.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(l) The Authority shall collect and publish information with respect to communications services.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(n) The Authority shall receive and investigate complaints relating to communications services and to take the necessary action.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(o) The Authority shall carry out projects to promote the development of the communications sector.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(p) The Authority shall provide technical support and represent the Government at international communications fora.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(q) The Authority shall ensure compliance with national and international communications standards and obligations laid down by international communication agreements and treaties to which Malawi is a party and to issue certificates of compliance in relation thereto.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(r) The Authority shall ensure the safety and quality of service for communications services.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(s) The Authority shall protect public health and safety.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(t) The Authority shall negotiate with the relevant Ministries, local authorities and private land owners with respect to rights of way.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(u) The Authority shall render technical assistance on communication matters to the Minister.

6. Duties, functions and powers of the Authority (2)(v) The Authority shall generally implement the objectives of this Act and do all such things as are necessary, incidental or conducive to the better carrying out of the functions of the Authority.

24. Confidentiality (3) This section shall apply to any information that— (a) a member of the Authority, employee, consultant, adviser or subcontractor of the Authority holds by virtue of his office or dealings with the Authority; (b) would not be expected, or would not be reasonable for it, to be disclosed by a member of the Authority, employee, consultant, adviser or subcontractor of the Authority except in the proper performance of the functions of his office; or (c) the member of the Authority, employee, consultant, adviser or subcontractor of the Authority holding the information knows or ought to know that it is unpublished information in relation to any contract or proposed contract of the Authority.

24. Confidentiality (4) The provisions of this section shall continue to apply to any member of the Authority, employee, consultant, adviser or subcontractor of the Authority, notwithstanding the expiry or termination of the term of office of the member or the employment of the employee, consultant, adviser or subcontractor of the Authority, as the case may be.

31. Requirement of licence for provision of electronic communications services (2) Notwithstanding section 33, a frequency spectrum licence shall be required in addition to any electronic communications licence where the operation of an electronic communications network or the provision of an electronic communications service entails the use of radio frequency.

33. Exemptions (1) The Authority may, in consultation with the Minister, exempt an entity or class of entities, for a period of time, from some of the requirements and obligations relating to an individual licence or a class licence issued under this Act.

33. Exemptions (2) The Authority shall make rules providing for the criteria of eligibility and application process, for exemptions.

33. Exemptions (3) The Authority may, where necessary, reverse its decision made under subsection (1), and in the event of such reversal, the Authority shall provide reasonable notice to the affected entity.

34. Licence eligibility The Authority shall not issue a licence under this Act unless the applicant for the licence is duly registered under the relevant written laws of Malawi.

36. Publication of rules relating to type or category of licence (1) The Authority shall publish in the Gazette and any other media, rules relating to each type or category of licence that may be issued under this Act.

36. Publication of rules relating to type or category of licence (2) Where a type or category of a licence is not provided for in the rules, the Authority shall, within a reasonable time, adopt provisional conditions allowing a person to start providing an electronic communications service, refuse the licence application, or exempt the person from the requirement of obtaining the licence.

37. Application for a licence (3) The Authority shall, within sixty days of receiving an application, grant or reject the application for a licence.

37. Application for a licence (4) The Authority shall, where an application for a licence is rejected, inform the applicant accordingly in writing, giving reasons why the application was rejected.

38. Grant of a licence through a restricted procedure (1) The Authority shall, where the operation of an electronic communications services requires the allocation of scarce resources, grant a licence through a restricted procedure in accordance with this Act.

38. Grant of a licence through a restricted procedure (2) Where the Authority considers that the granting of a licence should be subject to a restricted procedure, the choice of the successful applicant shall be based, as far as reasonably possible, on objective criteria made known to all applicants in advance.

39. Issue of a licence (a) The Authority shall issue a licence under this Act if the application complies with the provision of this Act.

39. Issue of a licence (b) The Authority shall issue a licence under this Act if the applicant is financially and technically capable of meeting his obligations and the terms and conditions of the licence.

40. General licence conditions (1)(a) A licence issued by the Authority shall be issued on payment by the applicant of the appropriate initial licence fees.

40. General licence conditions (1)(b) A licence issued by the Authority shall state the terms and conditions on which it is issued.

40. General licence conditions (1)(c) A licence issued by the Authority shall specify the services that may be provided.

40. General licence conditions (1)(d) A licence issued by the Authority shall come into effect upon publication in the Gazette.

40. General licence conditions (2) The Authority shall ensure that all licences issued under this Act have standard terms and conditions in respect of the category or type of the licence.

41. Amendment of a licence (1)(a) The Authority may amend a licence if it is necessary in the interest of the efficient management of the communications sector and provided that the amendment shall not cause substantial prejudice to the licensee.

41. Amendment of a licence (1)(d) The Authority may amend a licence with the agreement of the licensee.

41. Amendment of a licence (2) Where the Authority intends to amend a licence, it shall publish the proposed amendment in the Gazette for public comments and allow not less than thirty days from the date of the publication to give an opportunity to any person with an interest in the matter to make representations.

41. Amendment of a licence (3) Upon receipt of the representations on amendment of the licence under this section, the Authority shall consider the representations and determine whether to effect the amendment or not.

42. Transfer, assignment, etc., of a licence or rights thereunder (2) Subject to subsection (1), a licensee shall not transfer, assign, pledge or otherwise dispose of the licence or any rights unless the licensee has rolled out its services.

42. Transfer, assignment, etc., of a licence or rights thereunder (3) A licensee shall not transfer, assign, pledge or otherwise dispose of its licence or any rights thereunder, without the prior written consent of the Authority, which consent may be given subject to such reasonable terms and conditions as the Authority may impose, provided that the Authority shall not unreasonably withhold the consent.

42. Transfer, assignment, etc., of a licence or rights thereunder (4) A licensee may apply to the Authority in the prescribed manner for consent to transfer, assign, pledge or otherwise dispose of its licence or any rights thereunder.

42. Transfer, assignment, etc., of a licence or rights thereunder (5) An application made under subsection (4) shall be accompanied by an application for grant of a licence by the person to whom the licensee intends to transfer the licence.

42. Transfer, assignment, etc., of a licence or rights thereunder (6) The Authority shall, in considering an application for the transfer of a licence, have regard to the same terms and conditions as those that apply to the grant of a new licence, but the Authority may, in its discretion, refuse to grant the application.

42. Transfer, assignment, etc., of a licence or rights thereunder (8) The Authority shall grant or refuse to grant its consent to transfer a licence or any rights thereunder, within thirty days from the date of application.

42. Transfer, assignment, etc., of a licence or rights thereunder (9) Where consent is not granted, the Authority shall, within fourteen days from the date a decision not to grant the consent was made, provide a written explanation to the applicant, giving reasons for not granting the consent.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(a) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the licensee has failed to comply with this Act or the terms and conditions of his licence.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(b) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the licensee has contravened the provisions of any other written law relevant to the communications sector.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(c) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the licensee has failed to comply with any rules or lawful orders given by the Authority.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(d)(ii) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the licensee takes any action for its voluntary winding-up or dissolution.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(d)(iii) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the licensee enters into any scheme of arrangement, other than in any such case for the purpose of reconstruction or amalgamation, upon terms and within a period as may previously have been approved in writing by the Authority.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(d)(iv) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the licensee is the subject of any order made by a competent court or tribunal for its compulsory winding-up or dissolution.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(e) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the licensee has ceased, however briefly, to fulfil the eligibility requirements under this Act.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (1)(f) The Authority may suspend or revoke a licence granted under this Act if the suspension or revocation is in the public interest.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (2) Before the suspension or revocation of a licence under this section, the Authority shall inform the licensee by written notice, as soon as practicable, of its intention to suspend or revoke the licence and the reasons thereof.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (3) The affected licensee shall be given a reasonable opportunity to make written submissions to the Authority within a period specified in the notice, which period shall not be less than fourteen days from the date of the notice.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (4) The affected licensee may, within the period specified in the notice, make written submissions to the Authority and the Authority shall consider such submissions before making a determination in relation to the suspension or revocation of the licence.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (5) Subject to subsections (3) and (4) of this section, the suspension or revocation of a licence shall take effect on the expiry of thirty days from the date on which the notice of the Authority’s determination under subsection (4) of this section is served on the licensee.

43. Suspension or revocation of a licence (6) When the suspension or revocation of a licence has taken effect, the Authority shall, as soon as practicable, cause the suspension or revocation to be published in at least one national daily newspaper.

44. Application for renewal of a licence (2) An application for the renewal of the licence under subsection (1) shall be made in the prescribed manner upon payment of the prescribed fees.

44. Application for renewal of a licence (3) In considering an application for a renewal of a licence, the Authority shall have regard to whether— (a) the licensee performed in accordance with the obligations of his licence; (b) the licensee continues to meet the eligibility requirements under this Act; (c) the licensee continues to be financially and technically capable of meeting his obligations under this Act and any other related laws; and (d) the licensee has not, during the validity period of his licence, contravened the provisions of this Act, the terms and conditions of the licence, the rules issued by the Authority or any other relevant laws and regulations.

45. Obligation to roll out (1) Subject to subsection (3), a licensee shall be required to roll out its network and provide services within the period prescribed by the Authority in the licence.

45. Obligation to roll out (2) Where a licensee fails to roll out within the prescribed period, the licence shall be deemed to be automatically revoked.

45. Obligation to roll out (3) The Authority may extend the roll out period referred to in subsection (1)— (a) only once; (b) for a period not longer than the initial roll out period; and (c) only on grounds of a force majeure event as accepted by the Authority.

46. Individual licence (1) The Authority may grant individual licence for the operation of electronic communications network or the provision of electronic communications network.

46. Individual licence (2) The Authority shall classify an individual licence under the categories set out in the First Schedule.

47. Scope of a class licence The Authority shall issue a class licence authorizing the operation or provision of electronic communications services, save for licences connected with any of the following— (a) use of frequency spectrum; (b) use of numbers; (c) operation of electronic communications networks or facilities; or (d) any other activities that the Authority may determine from time to time.

48. Class licence to state terms and conditions of issuance A class licence issued by the Authority shall state the terms and conditions upon which the licence is issued.

49. Sub-classification The Authority may, from time to time, sub-classify communication services under this Act, and such sub-classification shall be published in the Gazette.

50. Registration under a class licence (1) An applicant for a class licence shall, prior to undertaking an activity applied for, complete the appropriate registration form for the class licence and file it with the Authority.

52. Licence fees (1)(a) A licensee shall pay the Authority such fees as may be prescribed by the Authority in accordance with this Act, and such fees shall include an initial licence fee payable before the licence is issued.

52. Licence fees (1)(b) A licensee shall pay the Authority such fees as may be prescribed by the Authority in accordance with this Act, and such fees shall include an annual licence fee.

52. Licence fees (1)(c) A licensee shall pay the Authority such fees as may be prescribed by the Authority in accordance with this Act, and such fees shall include licence renewal fees.

52. Licence fees (1)(d) A licensee shall pay the Authority such fees as may be prescribed by the Authority in accordance with this Act, and such fees shall include, where applicable, a fee in respect of any scarce resource.

52. Licence fees (1)(e) A licensee shall pay the Authority such fees as may be prescribed by the Authority in accordance with this Act, and such fees shall include any other fees or levies, including, but not limited to, the contribution to the Universal Service Fund.

53. Provision of essential services (1)(a) The Authority may, by notice published in the Gazette, require any licensee to provide emergency services, with priority routing, enabling any member of the public to make contact with, in particular, the police, the fire brigade and the ambulance service.

53. Provision of essential services (1)(b) The Authority may, by notice published in the Gazette, require any licensee to provide directory services, enabling any subscriber to obtain, through automated means, a telephone number listed in the telephone directory.

53. Provision of essential services (1)(c) The Authority may, by notice published in the Gazette, require any licensee to provide customer care services, enabling any subscriber to obtain assistance regarding matters such as access to services, setting up calls and remedying faults.

53. Provision of essential services (1)(d) The Authority may, by notice published in the Gazette, require any licensee to provide such other services as may be determined by the Authority from time to time by notice published in the Gazette.

53. Provision of essential services (2)(a) A licensee shall provide emergency service free of charge.

53. Provision of essential services (2)(c) A licensee shall provide such other services as may be determined by the Authority by notice published in the Gazette free of charge.

54. Protection of subscriber information (1) Subject to any written law or an order issued by the High Court on an inter partes hearing, a communications service licensee shall not disclose subscriber information which is in its custody.

55. Promotion of fair competition (1) The Authority shall, in the performance of its functions under this Act, promote, develop and enforce fair competition and equality of treatment among operators in any business or service relating to the communications service sector.

55. Promotion of fair competition (2) In the exercise of its powers under this Part, the Authority shall coordinate with the Competition and Fair Trading Commission established under the Competition and Fair Trading Act.

56. Regulation of interconnexion, access, etc. The Authority shall regulate the implementation of interconnexion, access, co-location and infrastructure sharing, based on the catalogues, interconnexion agreements and framed tariffs enforced by licensees.

57. Market analyses and dominant market players (2) The Authority shall, in consultation with the Competition and Fair Trading Commission and within twelve months of the commencement of this Act, issue rules defining the criteria to be used when determining a dominant position in a relevant market.

57. Market analyses and dominant market players (3)(a) A licensee shall be deemed as holding a dominant position in a relevant market if the licensee holds a large market share.

57. Market analyses and dominant market players (3)(c) A licensee shall be deemed as holding a dominant position in a relevant market if the licensee is involved in a vertical relationship that could harm competition in the market applicable to the particular category of licence as determined by the Authority.

57. Market analyses and dominant market players (3)(d) A licensee shall be deemed as holding a dominant position in a relevant market if any other factors as determined by the Authority apply.

57. Market analyses and dominant market players (6) The Authority may, by giving notice of not less than twenty-eight days and published in the Gazette, amend, for the remainder of a calendar year concerned, any list published pursuant to subsection (1).

58. Obligations for dominant market players (c) The Authority shall require any licensee found to be a dominant market player to modify any offers submitted under paragraph (b) in order to make the offers compliant with this Act;

